insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-line-sizing-engine-does', 1, 'A gathering system has four flowlines arriving at one header, and how the flow divides between them is unknown. What does this engine do with that problem?', '["Nothing, because it sizes one line and nothing in it knows another line exists. A system of lines sharing a header is a network solve and lives in engines/production/networkSolve.js.", "It sizes the four in turn and then balances them, iterating over the four rates until the pressures the four lines report at the shared header agree with each other to a stated tolerance.", "It refuses, returning an object carrying an error string, because a shared header is one of the eighteen states Section 1 lists as having no answer in this method.", "It sizes the four separately and adds the four pressure drops together, and that sum is the header pressure a network solve would have returned for the same system."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine takes a rate per line and returns what that one pipe spends. On OGBIA that is 25.660631 psi at 2.244621 ft/s, and every input on the call describes the one pipe.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-line-sizing-engine-does', 2, 'Where does two-phase pressure drop live, and what does this engine do wherever a holdup is needed?', '["It lives in this engine''s own flow-regime routine, which settles the regime first and then computes the holdup from it before any pressure drop is formed.", "It lives in engines/production/networkSolve.js beside the header balance, and the holdup reaches this engine as a returned value from that solve rather than as an argument.", "It lives in the Suite Beggs and Brill correlation, which is app code, and the engine takes the holdup as an INPUT wherever it needs one.", "It lives in the Suite correlation, and this engine calls out to it and keeps the holdup it gets back, so a caller never has to supply one of its own."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The multiphase half is not in this engine at all. A holdup arriving as an argument is a measurement or an assumption somebody else made, and the answer inherits it whole.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-line-sizing-engine-does', 3, 'A caller needs to know whether a liquid line call succeeded. What does this engine oblige it to do, and why?', '["Wrap the call in a try and a catch, because a state the method has no answer for is raised as an exception while an input that is simply absent comes back as data.", "Check a property on the object that came back, because every refusal is a returned object carrying an error string and the engine throws nothing at all.", "Compare the returned total against zero psi, because a refusal comes back with all three of its pressure terms at 0.000000 and is told apart by the arithmetic rather than by a property.", "Read the regime the call reports, because a refused liquid line returns its regime as an empty string while the rest of its fields are missing from the object entirely."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine returns { error: "liquid line drop needs positive rate, bore, length, density and viscosity" } rather than raising anything, so one code path carries both a result and a refusal.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-line-sizing-engine-does', 4, 'A liquid line call comes back with the message that it needs positive rate, bore, length, density and viscosity. How much has the caller been told about which input was at fault?', '["That the rate was at fault, since the message lists the five inputs in the order the guard tests them and the offending one is always named first.", "That exactly one of the five was missing, since a value that arrived negative produces a separate message naming the sign rather than this one.", "That the bore, the length and the density were all usable, since the message names only the inputs the guard could not accept and leaves out the ones it could.", "Nothing about which one. Five of the eighteen states Section 1 lists produce that identical string, and it states the contract the five inputs share rather than pointing at the offender."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A missing rate, a negative rate, a missing bore, a missing length and a missing viscosity all return that one string. The message describes the contract and the caller still has to read the row.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-line-sizing-engine-does', 5, 'Why does a liquid line with no viscosity produce a refusal rather than a default?', '["Because a defaulted centipoise would give a Reynolds number, a friction factor and a pressure drop that all print perfectly normally and all rest on a number nobody supplied.", "Because the viscosity is the one input of the five with no physically meaningful default, while a rate of zero and a length of zero are both taken as legal degenerate lines.", "Because the guard runs after the Reynolds number has been formed, so by the time the engine can refuse it has already discovered that the value it was handed was unusable.", "Because a substituted viscosity would be caught further down at the erosional check, which measures the line velocity against a ceiling that the density and the viscosity together settle."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A missing rate, bore, length, density or viscosity all return the same refusal. Refusing removes a whole class of quiet wrongness by declining to produce the first link in the chain.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-line-sizing-engine-does', 6, 'The OGBIA line carries 12000.000000 bpd. At what conditions is that rate taken, and what goes wrong if it is read as a stock tank figure?', '["At standard conditions, so the engine takes it to line conditions itself using the density of 54.500000 lb/ft3 before the flow area is divided into it to make a velocity.", "At line conditions upstream of separation, where the crude still carries gas in solution, which is why the 2.500000 cp is quoted as a live-oil viscosity at those conditions.", "At LINE conditions, the dead-liquid case downstream of separation, so it is 12000.000000 bpd of liquid in the pipe. Read as a tank figure waiting to shrink it puts the wrong volume through the area.", "At whatever conditions the caller states, because the engine reads a conditions flag supplied beside the rate and scales the volume from it before anything else runs."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A live-oil flowline upstream of separation carries full PVT and belongs elsewhere. Here 12000.000000 bpd of 54.500000 lb/ft3 crude is what is actually moving through the bore.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-line-sizing-engine-does', 7, 'Liquid work in this engine is stated in feet and gas work in miles. What does the engine itself say about the boundary between the two units?', '["Nothing at all, because the mile is a comment written beside the code rather than a live constant, so there is no question a caller can ask the engine that would measure it.", "It accepts a rise of 5280.000000000 ft on a line 1.000000 mile long and refuses anything above 26400.000000 ft, which is exactly the five miles the OGBIA liquid line happens to run to.", "It converts miles to feet on the liquid side as well, so a liquid line handed 5280.000000000 ft and one handed 1.000000 mile both come back with the same 25.660631 psi.", "Asked for the largest rise it will accept on a gas line 1.000000 mile long, it accepts 5280.000000000 ft and refuses the next representable value above that, 9.095e-13 ft higher."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The mile is a measured boundary in the code rather than a note beside it. The OGBIA liquid line is stated as 26400.000000 ft, and the engine wants feet there.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-line-sizing-engine-does', 8, 'gc is reported as 32.174000 lbm ft per lbf s2. How was that figure obtained for this digest?', '["By reading it off the module, which exports gc along with the other constants the chain needs so that a caller can reproduce any step of the arithmetic by hand.", "By measuring it out of the engine, as the density times the velocity squared over twice 144 times the fittings loss at a resistance sum of one, from 2.244621 ft/s and 0.029634 psi.", "By typing the published value, since gc is a definition of the pound rather than anything an engine computes, so no question that could be put to the engine would ever return the figure itself.", "By dividing the friction loss of 25.660631 psi by the velocity head at a resistance sum of one, which cancels the length and the friction factor between them and leaves gc standing on its own."]'::jsonb, 1, 'gc reconciles the pound as a mass with the pound as a force. Isolating it needs a call whose only loss is the fittings term, which is why the resistance sum of one is used.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-line-sizing-engine-does', 9, 'Why does this digest measure gc, the centipoise, the barrel and the day out of the engine rather than printing their published values?', '["Because the engine''s own values differ slightly from the published ones, and what the chain actually used is what a reader has to be given to reproduce any figure in it.", "Because each of the four is a derived group with no published value behind it anywhere, so measuring it out of the engine is the only route to any figure at all.", "Because the module keeps those constants to itself, so a number typed into a teaching file would not be an engine return.", "Because the module exports them under names this digest has no way to import, so asking the engine a question about itself is how a present but unreachable value is recovered."]'::jsonb, 2, 'One centipoise comes back as 6.7197000000e-4 lbm per ft per s and one barrel as 5.6145833333333 cubic feet, each recovered by choosing inputs that isolate it.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-line-sizing-engine-does', 10, 'Working the OGBIA chain from the bore to the loss, which single step waits on anything, and what does that mean for the rest?', '["Only the friction factor, which iterates. Everything else substitutes, and the friction factor hands back a settled 0.0218149625 that the rest of the chain then uses as an ordinary input.", "The velocity, because the rate has to pass through the barrel and the day before an area can be divided into it, and that conversion runs to a tolerance of its own.", "The Reynolds number, because it needs the velocity and the velocity needs the area, so the two have to be solved against each other before either can be reported.", "None of them, because the friction factor is looked up on the Moody chart at the Reynolds number and the relative roughness rather than being solved for."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The area is arithmetic, the velocity is a division and the Reynolds number is a product of four settled figures. Nothing in the tier waits except that one step.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-line-sizing-engine-does', 11, 'A report quotes 25.660631 psi for OGBIA crude. What is wrong with carrying that figure across to another line carrying the same crude?', '["Nothing is wrong with it, provided the second line carries the same 12000.000000 bpd, because the loss is settled once the rate and the fluid properties are settled.", "It needs rescaling by the ratio of the two lengths and by nothing else, since the length is the only term in the friction loss that a second line is free to change.", "It needs rescaling by the ratio of the two bores and by nothing else, since the bore reaches the friction loss exactly once, through the length over bore factor that stands in front of the velocity head.", "It belongs to a whole row rather than to the fluid: that rate, that bore, that length and that wall. The same duty through 6.065000 in spends 97.306913 psi and the crude did nothing."]'::jsonb, 3, '12000.000000 bpd through 7.981000 in spends 25.660631 psi and through 6.065000 in spends 97.306913 psi. The method travels between lines and the numbers do not.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-line-sizing-engine-does', 12, 'On the liquid side, one of the states Section 1 lists is about the shape of the input rather than about a physical impossibility. Which is it, and how does it arrive?', '["A liquid line handed a negative rate, and it arrives as a thrown exception, because a sign error is a fault in the calling code rather than a state the method has no answer for.", "A traverse handed no profile segment, and it arrives exactly as the others do, as an object carrying { error: \"a traverse needs at least one profile segment\" }.", "A wall specified to a design code that does not exist, and it arrives as a null return so that a caller can test for it without having to read and compare a message string.", "A pig that does not move, and it arrives as a returned run time of zero hours, which a caller reads as the line having no length for the sphere to cross."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Whether the method was handed nonsense or handed a state it cannot size, the return is an object, the property is error, and the string says what was wanted.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-line-sizing-engine-does', 13, 'A liquid line''s length is handed over in miles where the engine asked for feet. What in the returned numbers reveals it?', '["The gradient, which is the total loss divided by the length and would come back stated in psi per mile, so the unit printed beside it gives the swap away immediately.", "The regime, which flips to laminar because a length thousands of times too small drags the Reynolds number down below the 2100 the engine leaves the laminar branch at.", "Nothing. Both are lengths and both are ordinary numbers, the loss scales directly with the length, and the answer comes back wrong by whatever the confusion was worth.", "The refusal, since a line whose length is implausibly short against its own bore trips the guard that compares an elevation change against the length of the line."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The friction loss is the friction factor times the length over the bore times the velocity head, so the length scales it directly and nothing downstream is placed to object.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-line-sizing-engine-does', 14, 'What does a line-sizing answer from this engine cover, and what does it leave out?', '["It says what one pipe costs in pressure and what bore it needs. It does not say how a header full of pipes divides a flow, and it does not say what happens when gas and liquid travel together.", "It covers the pipe and the header at once, since the rate the caller hands over has already accounted for the split, so a single-line answer is a network answer stated one line at a time.", "It covers a two-phase line as well, provided a holdup is supplied, because the holdup is the one quantity a multiphase pressure drop needs beyond the single-phase chain.", "It covers a live-oil flowline as readily as a dead-liquid one, because the density and the viscosity are both supplied at line conditions in either case."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Those are different methods. The network solve is another engine and the two-phase work is the Suite correlation, which this engine never calls.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-line-sizing-engine-does', 15, 'What does a rate in bpd need before it is a velocity in ft/s?', '["The barrel and the day alone, since the engine holds its flow area in square feet already and the bore is never taken out of the inches it arrived in.", "The barrel, the day and the density of 54.500000 lb/ft3 as well, since a volumetric rate only becomes a velocity once the mass the stream is carrying has been settled and then divided back out of it.", "The barrel, the day and gc at 32.174000 lbm ft per lbf s2, since gc reconciles the pound as a mass with the pound as a force in every field-unit conversion this engine makes anywhere at all.", "The barrel of 5.6145833333333 cubic feet, the 86400.000000 seconds in a day, and a flow area, which wants the bore in feet and so brings in the 144 square inches in a square foot."]'::jsonb, 3, '12000.000000 bpd through a flow area of 0.347410 ft2 gives 2.244621 ft/s. Miss any one of the three and the figure still prints in ft/s and still feeds a Reynolds number.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-velocity-reynolds-and-friction', 1, 'A bore of 7.981000 in is handed to the engine. What does it become before any physics happens, and what does the conversion need?', '["A relative roughness of 0.0002255356, which needs the commercial steel roughness of 0.001800 in, since the bore reaches the chain first as a fraction of the wall height rather than as an area at all.", "A flow area of 0.347410 ft2, which needs the 144 square inches in a square foot because the bore arrives in inches and the area is wanted in square feet.", "A velocity of 2.244621 ft/s directly, which needs the barrel and the day, because the engine divides the rate by the bore and never forms an area.", "A hydraulic diameter in feet, which needs only a division by twelve, and the area then follows from it inside the friction term."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The bore encloses 0.347410 ft2, and a bore twice as wide encloses four times the area. That square is why bore changes move a velocity further than they look as though they should.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-velocity-reynolds-and-friction', 2, 'The viscosity of the OGBIA crude is walked from 1.000000 cp to 120.000000 cp at the built bore. What does the velocity column do?', '["It falls steadily from 2.244621 ft/s, because a thicker fluid meets more resistance at the wall and the same pressure difference therefore pushes less of it through the same bore each second.", "It falls only once the flow goes laminar at 60.000000 cp, because below the branch the velocity profile changes shape and the mean velocity drops away from the value the turbulent rows carried.", "It rises slowly, because the friction factor climbs from 0.0185483924 to 0.0634301170 across the walk and a larger friction factor drives a larger velocity head and so a faster stream.", "It holds at 2.244621 ft/s on every row."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Velocity is the rate over the area and neither of those is the viscosity. Everything downstream of the Reynolds number moves, and the Reynolds number itself falls from 121078.1307 to 1008.9844.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-velocity-reynolds-and-friction', 3, 'The Reynolds number takes four inputs. Which of them enters the chain at this step and nowhere earlier, and what does that make this step?', '["The viscosity. The density, the velocity and the bore are all settled before it, so this is where the fluid''s character finally reaches the calculation.", "The density, since the velocity step is a rate and a geometry that would return 2.244621 ft/s for water or for crude alike, so the fluid arrives here.", "The bore, since the velocity was formed from the rate and the flow area rather than from the bore itself, so 7.981000 in reaches the arithmetic for the first time in this expression.", "The velocity, since the caller supplied a rate and 2.244621 ft/s is computed inside the Reynolds step from the rate and the area."]'::jsonb, 0, 'On OGBIA the four are 54.500000 lb/ft3, 2.244621 ft/s, 7.981000 in and 2.500000 cp, giving 48431.2523. The density and the bore both entered earlier.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-velocity-reynolds-and-friction', 4, 'A viscosity of 2.500000 cp has to be converted before the Reynolds number can use it. What is it converted to, and what does the Reynolds number itself carry as a unit?', '["To a kinematic viscosity in ft2 per s by dividing by the density of 54.500000 lb/ft3, and the Reynolds number then carries ft2 per s, which is what lets it be compared against a fixed boundary.", "To lbm per ft per s, and the Reynolds number carries that same unit forward, which is why the boundaries are stated for field units.", "To 6.7197000000e-4 lbm per ft per s for one centipoise, and the Reynolds number carries no unit at all, because every unit in it cancels.", "To centistokes, and the Reynolds number is dimensionless only on the turbulent branch, because the laminar law divides by a numerator of 64.000000 that carries units of its own."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The conversion is measured out of the engine rather than typed in. Being dimensionless is precisely what lets one Reynolds number be compared against fixed boundaries for any fluid in any pipe.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-velocity-reynolds-and-friction', 5, 'Below a Reynolds number of 2100 the engine uses the laminar law. What does that law contain, and what is it blind to?', '["The Reynolds number and the relative roughness, as the turbulent law does, but with the roughness term multiplied by a numerator of 64.000000 that damps the wall''s contribution to almost nothing.", "The Reynolds number and nothing else. No roughness appears in it, so a coated pipe and a used steel pipe of the same bore return the same friction factor.", "The Reynolds number and the velocity head, and it is blind to the length, which is why a laminar friction factor can be quoted for a pipe before the length of that pipe has been decided at all.", "The Reynolds number and the bore, and it is blind to the density, since in layered flow only the viscous term is left."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The laminar numerator is measured out of the engine as the friction factor times its own Reynolds number, giving 64.000000 from 0.064000000000 at 1000.0000. In layered flow the bumps have nothing to disturb.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-velocity-reynolds-and-friction', 6, 'A published case puts 150.0000 bpd through 2.067000 in over 5000.000000 ft. Why does so small a duty cost 130.867123 psi when OGBIA''s 12000.000000 bpd costs 25.660631 psi?', '["Because a narrow bore and a crawling velocity of 0.418299 ft/s put it at a Reynolds number of 15.5476, deep on the laminar branch, where the friction factor is 4.1163843599.", "Because the shorter line concentrates the whole loss into 5000.000000 ft, and the friction loss is inversely proportional to the length, so a shorter pipe at the same duty always spends more.", "Because the small bore drives the relative roughness up, and on the laminar branch the wall term dominates the friction factor completely below about three inches.", "Because the low rate leaves the line only partly full, so the engine falls back on a holdup of its own and uses a reduced flow area."]'::jsonb, 0, 'That case is the only one of the four published liquid cases whose friction factor is above one. Velocity and cost move together on the turbulent branch, and a line slow enough to go laminar breaks that habit.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-velocity-reynolds-and-friction', 7, 'Why is the turbulent friction factor described as solved rather than evaluated?', '["Because the relative roughness of 0.0002255356 has to be found first by trial against the bore, and only once that fraction has settled can the friction factor itself be substituted directly.", "Because the Reynolds number and the friction factor are solved against each other, since the Reynolds number depends on the velocity and the velocity depends on the loss the friction factor produces.", "Because the engine reads it from the Moody chart, and reading a chart between two plotted roughness curves is an interpolation rather than a substitution of numbers into a formula.", "Because it appears on both sides of the expression, so the engine iterates until the value stops moving."]'::jsonb, 3, 'It is the one step in the tier that does not simply substitute. It converges to the oracle digit for digit at 0.037504518014, 0.021926421295, 0.019943465840 and 0.010859745054.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-velocity-reynolds-and-friction', 8, 'The absolute roughness of commercial steel is 0.001800 in. What does the correlation actually read, and what follows from that?', '["The absolute figure itself in inches, since the correlation was fitted against measured wall heights and converts to a fraction internally once the Reynolds number has selected a branch for it.", "The roughness over the flow area of 0.347410 ft2, which makes it dimensionless and the same steel four times rougher in half the bore.", "A relative roughness of 0.0002255356, the roughness over the bore of 7.981000 in, so the same steel in a narrower bore is hydraulically rougher.", "The roughness multiplied by the Colebrook divisor of 3.700000, which is the form the published correlation states, and the bore enters separately through the Reynolds number instead."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The pipe reaches the friction factor as a proportion rather than as a length. The same bumps occupy more of a narrower passage, so 0.006000 in of used steel gives 0.0007517855.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-velocity-reynolds-and-friction', 9, 'Read the roughness table''s two columns, at Reynolds 100000.0000 and at 100000000.0000, against each other. What do they say?', '["Both rows fall by about the same proportion, since raising the Reynolds number by a thousandfold lowers every friction factor in the same way whatever the wall is made of.", "A rough pipe stops caring about the Reynolds number and a smooth one never does. At a relative roughness of 0.050000 the two columns are 0.071780929441 and 0.071550904091, and at 0.000000 they are 0.017989773084 and 0.005940466352.", "The rough row falls furthest, because a larger relative roughness leaves more room for the Reynolds term to work on, and the smooth row is already close to the floor the correlation allows.", "The two columns agree on the smooth row and separate on the rough one, since a smooth pipe has no wall term at all and so nothing in it can respond to a change in the Reynolds number."]'::jsonb, 1, 'That table is the vertical axis of the Moody chart. On the roughest row the wall has taken over, so the loss follows the velocity head alone, and on the smoothest the answer stays sensitive to the rate.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-velocity-reynolds-and-friction', 10, 'Between Reynolds 2100 and 4000 the engine reports the regime as transitional. What is it computing there?', '["A blend of the two laws weighted by where the Reynolds number sits in the band, which is why the interior rows fall smoothly from 0.048678586645 at 2100.0000 to 0.043519188769 at 3000.0000.", "A third correlation fitted to the transition band itself, which is what the label is announcing, and it is the reason the values in the band do not continue either neighbouring branch.", "The laminar law still, since the engine only leaves that branch at 4000, and the transitional label marks the stretch where the laminar answer is reported with a warning attached to it.", "The turbulent law. The word and the arithmetic are saying different things."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The lower value at 2099.0000 is reported as laminar and the upper at 2100.0000 as transitional, and the upper one is computed on the turbulent branch. Reading the label as a third correlation reads something that is not there.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-velocity-reynolds-and-friction', 11, 'At the OGBIA relative roughness the friction factor steps across the lower boundary. What is the step, and between which two values?', '["A jump of 60.303955 percent, from 0.0304761905 to 0.0488545386 across one unit of Reynolds number.", "A jump of 60.303955 percent, from 0.030490709862 to 0.048678586645, which are the values the branch walk prints at 2099.0000 and at 2100.0000 on that same line.", "A fall of 60.303955 percent, from 0.0488545386 down to 0.0304761905, because the turbulent law returns the smaller figure and the laminar law the larger one at that Reynolds number.", "A step of about a tenth of a percent, from 0.039909964901 to 0.039907014056, which is what one unit of Reynolds number is worth anywhere along the friction curve."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The pair at 0.030490709862 and 0.048678586645 belongs to a SMOOTH pipe, and the OGBIA pair at its own relative roughness of 0.0002255356 is the one that gives 60.303955 percent.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-velocity-reynolds-and-friction', 12, 'The upper boundary of the band runs from 3999.0000 to 4000.0000. How does the friction factor behave there, and why?', '["It jumps, as it does at the lower boundary, because the engine leaves one law and starts another one there.", "It falls sharply, because the turbulent law carries the roughness term while the transitional label suppresses it, so crossing into turbulent is where the wall first reaches the friction factor.", "It barely moves, from 0.039909964901 to 0.039907014056, because both rows were computed the same way and only the word changed.", "It cannot be read at all, since the engine reports the regime without a friction factor on that row and a caller has to compute the value itself from the Reynolds number and the relative roughness."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The discontinuity sits at 2100, where the engine changes branch. At 4000 the label changes and the arithmetic does not, so the two rows agree to four decimal places.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-velocity-reynolds-and-friction', 13, 'Raising the OGBIA viscosity from 40.000000 cp to 60.000000 cp moves the Reynolds number from 3026.9533 to 2017.9688. What happens to the friction loss?', '["It falls, from 51.289913 psi to 37.305974 psi, because the two rows were computed on different branches and the discontinuity between them produced it.", "It rises, from 51.289913 psi to 74.611948 psi, because a thicker oil in the same pipe at the same rate always meets more friction on the way and the laminar branch is where that is steepest.", "It rises slightly, because the loss is continuous across the branch even though the friction factor and the reported regime are not, so nothing in the pressure records the crossing.", "It is refused, because a Reynolds number inside the band from 2100 to 4000 has no honest correlation behind it."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A thicker oil in the same pipe at the same rate came out cheaper. That is the branch discontinuity showing up in a pressure, and it is the clearest reason to read the reported regime.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-velocity-reynolds-and-friction', 14, 'The two constants of the turbulent law are measured out of the engine. Where is each one isolated?', '["Both on the OGBIA line at a relative roughness of 0.0002255356, since that is the only condition for which the engine prints a friction factor to the twelve digits the measurement needs.", "The Reynolds numerator in the fully rough limit at a relative roughness of 0.010000, and the roughness divisor on a smooth pipe, since each constant is read where its own term is largest.", "Both at a Reynolds number of 100000.0000, by solving the two-unknown expression against the two friction factors 0.017989773084 and 0.037903711893 that the engine returns at that condition.", "The Reynolds numerator of 2.510000 on a SMOOTH pipe, where the roughness term is exactly zero, and the roughness divisor of 3.700000 in the FULLY ROUGH limit, where the Reynolds term falls away."]'::jsonb, 3, '2.510000 comes from 0.017989773084 at Reynolds 100000.0000 on a smooth pipe. 3.700000 comes from 0.037903711893 at a relative roughness of 0.010000 and a Reynolds number of 100000000000000.0000.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-velocity-reynolds-and-friction', 15, 'A line sits at a Reynolds number between the two branch boundaries and a value is wanted for it. What is the mistake to avoid?', '["Reporting the regime beside the value, since the label says transitional while the arithmetic is turbulent and the word misleads a later reader.", "Using the turbulent value at all, since the engine computes it on a branch fitted for a different regime, so the only defensible answer inside the band is the laminar one at the lower boundary.", "Interpolating between a laminar answer and a turbulent one, because the two are different laws and a value taken between them corresponds to nothing the method computes.", "Quoting the figure to the digits the engine gives, since an iterated value inside a band with no correlation carries fewer honest digits."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine steps from one law to the other at 2100 and does not blend them. The honest response is to take the number as the turbulent law''s opinion and to report the regime alongside it.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-three-losses-kept-apart', 1, 'A resistance sum of one on the OGBIA line costs 0.029634 psi. What is that quantity built from?', '["The friction factor of 0.0218149625 times the length over the bore, which is the group the fittings term shares with the pipe friction term and is where both of them get their pressure units.", "The density times the velocity over gc, with the 144 taking it to psi, so the head is proportional to the velocity itself.", "The density of 54.500000 lb/ft3 times the square of 2.244621 ft/s, over twice gc at 32.174000 lbm ft per lbf s2, and then over 144 to reach psi.", "The velocity of 2.244621 ft/s over the flow area of 0.347410 ft2, scaled by the barrel and the day, which is the same conversion the velocity step ran and is why the two share a constant."]'::jsonb, 2, 'gc reconciles the pound as a mass with the pound as a force and the 144 takes the answer to psi. Both of the pipe''s pressure terms are that same square wearing different coefficients.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-three-losses-kept-apart', 2, 'A line is resized so that it runs twice as fast at the same duty. What happens to the velocity head, and what follows from it?', '["It is four times as large, because the velocity is squared, and both the friction loss and the fittings loss are built on it, so both follow the square.", "It doubles, because the head is proportional to the velocity, and the fittings loss is a fixed cost per fitting and stays where it was.", "It is four times as large, and the friction loss follows it, but the fittings loss does not, because a resistance sum counts fittings.", "It is unchanged, because the head is a property of the fluid at its density, and the resistance sum carries whatever the velocity is doing."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The friction loss is the velocity head times the friction factor times the length over the bore, and the fittings loss is the velocity head times the resistance sum. The square is what makes a fast line expensive.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-three-losses-kept-apart', 3, 'What is a resistance sum, and what does a fitting cost before a velocity is put through it?', '["A pressure drop in psi accumulated from the fitting table, so each fitting already carries its own cost and the sum of the list is the fittings loss the line will report.", "An equivalent length in feet, added to the pipe length before the friction term is formed, which is why the same isometric is worth more on a short run than on a long one.", "A count of bends and valves on the isometric, so it is an integer, and the pressure it costs follows from the count and the bore without the velocity entering it at all.", "A count of velocity heads, and nothing."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The OGBIA list comes to 4.500000 velocity heads and costs 0.133351 psi on that line. The count belongs to the shape of the fitting and the pressure belongs to the stream it is placed in.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-three-losses-kept-apart', 4, 'On the OGBIA isometric, which single fitting contributes the most resistance, and how does it compare with the rest of the list?', '["The four long radius elbows, whose 1.200000 in total is the largest entry on the list because they are the most numerous, and a bend turns the whole stream through a right angle.", "The swing check at 2.000000 velocity heads, which on its own is more than the four elbows contribute together at 1.200000.", "The sudden exit at 1.000000, since the stream loses the whole of its velocity head on leaving the pipe and no other fitting on the list can cost more than one entire head.", "The two gate valves at 0.300000 together, because a valve stem sits permanently in the flow path."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The four K totals are 1.200000, 0.300000, 2.000000 and 1.000000, summing to 4.500000. A check valve holds a disc in the path of the flow every second of the line''s life.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-three-losses-kept-apart', 5, 'The same fittings are placed on the full 26400.000000 ft line and on a 300.000000 ft manifold run at the same duty. What moved between the two cases?', '["The pipe. It spends 25.660631 psi on the long line and 0.291598 psi on the short one, while the fittings cost 0.133351 psi in both.", "The fittings. They cost 0.133351 psi on the long line and 0.313805 psi on the short one, because a shorter run puts them closer together and their disturbances no longer settle out between them.", "Both terms fell with the length, the pipe from 25.660631 psi to 0.291598 psi and the fittings from 0.133351 psi to 0.005170 psi.", "Neither term moved, and only the share changed, because the share is computed against the total rather than against the pipe friction and the total is what the shorter length altered."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The share runs from 0.005170 of the total on the long line to 0.313805 on the manifold run. The rule that follows is about the length: ask what the pipe is spending before deciding whether the isometric matters.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-three-losses-kept-apart', 6, 'The friction loss multiplies three things together. Which of them does the length sit in, and what follows?', '["The velocity head, since the head is the pressure the stream carries over the whole run of the pipe, so a longer line carries more of it.", "The friction factor, since Colebrook is solved over the length of the pipe and a longer line gives the iteration more wall to settle against.", "The length over bore factor, and nothing in the friction factor or the velocity head depends on how long the pipe is, so the loss follows the length directly.", "All three, since the length reaches the Reynolds number through the velocity as well, so doubling a line more than doubles the loss."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The same duty and the same fittings over 300.000000 ft spend 0.291598 psi of pipe friction against 25.660631 psi over 26400.000000 ft. That proportionality lets a loss be scaled along a route.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-three-losses-kept-apart', 7, 'The OGBIA line as built reports friction 25.660631 psi, fittings 0.000000 psi, elevation 0.000000 psi and a total of 25.660631 psi. What does the fittings term of zero mean?', '["That the engine could not read the isometric, so it returned the term as zero rather than refusing, which is the one place in the liquid chain where a missing input is defaulted instead of refused.", "That the fittings on the isometric are present but too small to register against 25.660631 psi of pipe friction, so the term rounds to zero at the six decimals the engine prints.", "That the fittings term is only returned when a hill is present, because the engine forms the fittings and elevation terms together in the same step and reports them as a pair.", "That the call carried a resistance sum of zero, which is a perfectly legal line with no fittings."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The published liquid cases carry resistance sums of 0.000000, 4.500000 and 2.000000, and the ones at zero return a fittings loss of 0.000000 psi. The term is present as a zero rather than as an absence.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-three-losses-kept-apart', 8, 'The OGBIA line is run level, then up 420.000000 ft, then down 420.000000 ft. Which column of the result does not move, and why?', '["The total column, which reads 25.660631 psi on all three rows, because the engine adds the static column to the inlet pressure instead.", "The friction column, which reads 25.660631 psi on all three rows, because the hill changed nothing about the rate, the bore, the roughness or the viscosity.", "The gradient column, which reads 0.0009719936 psi per ft on all three rows, because the gradient divides the friction term by the length and the elevation term is reported separately from it.", "The elevation column, which reads 158.958333 psi on all three rows, because the hill is the same 420.000000 ft in both tilted directions."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The elevation term is symmetric about zero at 0.000000, 158.958333 and -158.958333 psi. A hill is added to a pressure drop rather than mixed into it.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-three-losses-kept-apart', 9, 'A liquid line returns a total of -133.297702 psi. What is that describing?', '["A descent where the line gains more from the fall than it spends on friction, so the fluid arrives at a higher pressure than it left.", "A refusal expressed as a number, since the engine has no error channel on the total and reverses the sign instead.", "A line whose elevation change exceeds its own length, which is the state the engine guards against, reported as a negative total rather than as a message on this particular call.", "An arithmetic underflow in the elevation term, which is why only the combined figure carries the sign."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The friction term inside it is still positive and still real. The published case at 800.0000 bpd dropping 60.000000 ft does the same thing: 14.550645 psi of friction against -26.000000 psi of elevation.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-three-losses-kept-apart', 10, 'On the published case at 20000.0000 bpd through 10.020000 in over 52800.000000 ft, climbing 250.000000 ft, which of the three terms is the largest?', '["The friction, at 56.140803 psi, which is the usual situation on a long line because the friction term grows with the 52800.000000 ft while a static column depends only on the height.", "The fittings, at 0.153195 psi, once the resistance sum of 4.500000 is scaled by the velocity head of a line running at 2.373400 ft/s, which is faster than OGBIA and so carries a larger head.", "The elevation, at 97.222222 psi, against 56.140803 psi of friction and 0.153195 psi of fittings inside a total of 153.516220 psi.", "They cannot be ranked from the return, because the engine reports only the total of 153.516220 psi."]'::jsonb, 2, 'That is an ordinary situation on a route with relief in it, and it is the reason the engine declines to blend the terms. The answer to a friction problem and the answer to an elevation problem are different equipment.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-three-losses-kept-apart', 11, 'A total is dominated by its elevation term. What does buying a larger bore do about it?', '["It fixes it in proportion to the square of the bore, because the static column is the density times the height times the flow area, so a wider pipe spreads the same weight over more area.", "It fixes it as effectively as it fixes friction, since both terms are returned in psi and both are reduced by the fall in velocity that a larger bore produces at the same duty.", "It makes it worse, because a larger bore slows the stream and a slower stream spends longer under the full weight of the column, which is what the elevation term is measuring.", "Very little, because a wider pipe holds a wider column of exactly the same height."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Friction is what a bigger pipe fixes: open the bore, the velocity falls, the head falls with its square and the friction collapses. Elevation is what no pipe fixes.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-three-losses-kept-apart', 12, 'What is the gradient, and what does it do to the three terms the engine kept apart?', '["It is the friction loss divided by the length, so it reports the pipe''s own cost per foot and leaves the fittings and the elevation out of the figure entirely, which is what makes it comparable.", "It is the total loss divided by the length, so it divides their sum and puts back together what the engine separated.", "It is the total loss divided by the length in miles, which is the unit a route is costed in, and it is the one place in the liquid chain where the engine works in miles rather than feet.", "It is the outlet pressure divided by the inlet pressure, expressed per foot."]'::jsonb, 1, 'On the level OGBIA line the gradient is 0.0009719936 psi per ft. Nothing in a gradient on a tilted line says which of friction and elevation is doing the work.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-three-losses-kept-apart', 13, 'The level OGBIA line reports a gradient of 0.0009719936 psi per ft and the same line up 420.000000 ft reports 0.0069931426. What is the whole of the difference?', '["The hill. Both rows describe the same pipe carrying the same crude at the same velocity, and the friction term is identical on both.", "The change in velocity, since a climbing line runs slower against the static column and a slower stream sits at a different point on the friction curve, which the gradient then averages over.", "The fittings, since the isometric''s 4.500000 velocity heads are in the tilted total and left out of the level one.", "The length, since the engine measures a climbing line along the slope rather than along the ground."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The level row is 25.660631 psi and the tilted row is 184.618965 psi over the same 26400.000000 ft. Every bit of the difference between the two gradients is the 158.958333 psi of static column.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-three-losses-kept-apart', 14, 'A gradient comes back as -0.0050491554 psi per ft and is quoted as a magnitude. What has that done?', '["It has made no difference, since a gradient is an average over the whole length and the sign belongs to the elevation term rather than to the average, which is reported unsigned by convention.", "It has doubled the apparent loss, because dropping the sign adds the recovered static column to the friction instead of subtracting it.", "It has turned a line that arrives strong into a line that arrives weak, because the negative sign says the line gains pressure along its length.", "It has made the figure comparable with the level line''s 0.0009719936 psi per ft, which is how a gradient is usually quoted."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The downhill row totals -133.297702 psi over 26400.000000 ft. The sign is doing real work, and a gradient taken as a magnitude reverses what the line is actually doing.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-three-losses-kept-apart', 15, 'A line climbs and then descends and one average gradient is reported for it. Which question does that single figure still settle?', '["What the pressure is at a point in the middle, since an average over a uniform slope describes the pipe honestly at any station along it and a line with two slopes is two uniform slopes.", "What the friction term is worth on its own, since the fittings and the elevation are reported separately and the gradient is formed from the friction alone before the other two are added.", "Which of friction and elevation is doing the work, since a gradient above the level line''s 0.0009719936 psi per ft could only have been raised by a static column acting over the route.", "What the total loss over the whole route is."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The gradient is the total divided by the length, so multiplying it back recovers what it divided. It also puts the three terms back together, and on a line that climbs and then descends the middle is a separate question.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-erosional-limit', 1, 'The API RP 14E erosional velocity takes how many inputs, and which are they?', '["Three, being the c factor, the density and the bore, since a velocity ceiling has to know the passage it is a ceiling for before it can be stated as a number in ft per second.", "Four, being the c factor, the density, the viscosity and the rate, since erosion depends on how much fluid passes the wall as well as on how fast and how heavy that fluid is.", "Two, being the c factor and the mixture velocity, since the check compares a velocity against a ceiling and both halves of that comparison are velocities in the same units.", "Two: a c factor and the fluid density."]'::jsonb, 3, 'At the OGBIA crude density of 54.500000 lb/ft3 and a c factor of 100.000000 the ceiling is 13.545709 ft/s. No bore, no length, no roughness, no viscosity and no rate appear in it.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-erosional-limit', 2, 'Why can an erosional velocity be quoted before a bore has been chosen?', '["Because the engine assumes the smallest bore in the vendored schedule until a real one is supplied, which gives the most conservative ceiling any candidate bore could produce for that fluid.", "Because it is a ceiling on the fluid at its density, and the sizing question then becomes which bores keep the line underneath it.", "Because the ceiling is stated per unit of flow area, so it is quoted first as a rate per square foot and only becomes a velocity once a bore has fixed the 0.347410 ft2 it acts over.", "Because the c factor already carries a nominal bore inside it, which is what the three published rows differ by."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The expression contains the c factor and the density and nothing else. Which bores sit under 13.545709 ft/s is the sizing question, and the ceiling itself is settled before it is asked.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-erosional-limit', 3, 'The same c factor of 100.000000 is applied to fluids at 5.000000 and at 62.400000 lb/ft3. What is the ratio of the two allowances, and what sets it?', '["3.532704, which is the square root of the ratio of the two densities, so the allowance runs 44.721360 ft/s against 12.659242 ft/s.", "The ratio of the two densities themselves rather than its square root, so the light fluid is allowed to run proportionally faster in exactly the measure that it is lighter than the heavy one.", "The ratio of the widest published c factor to the narrowest, since the density settles which row applies.", "3.532704, which is the square of the ratio of the two densities, and squaring is what holds the change down when a density spans more than a factor of ten between two fluids."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The density sits under a root, so the allowance moves more slowly than the density does. Read down any column of the density table and the allowance falls as the fluid gets heavier.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-erosional-limit', 4, 'Reading ACROSS a row of the density table rather than down a column is reading what?', '["The density alone, since each column of the table is computed at a different density and the c factor is held at the continuous-service figure of 100.000000 throughout the whole table.", "Both at once, since the c factor and the density enter the expression together and a row crosses three services at three different densities as the table is laid out.", "The c factor alone, because it multiplies the whole expression and the density on that row does not change.", "Neither, since a row of the table is three separate calls with nothing in common, and only the diagonal of the table holds the density and the c factor in a fixed proportion to each other."]'::jsonb, 2, 'At 5.000000 lb/ft3 the allowance runs 44.721360, 55.901699 and 78.262379 ft/s across the three columns. Down a column the density is doing the work and across a row the service is.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-erosional-limit', 5, 'The module carries three c factor rows. What is the provenance of each?', '["All three are API RP 14E, at 100.000000, 125.000000 and 175.000000, with the third applying only to clean and inhibited duty.", "One is API RP 14E at 100.000000, and the other two at 125.000000 and 175.000000 are operator practice, so only they are overridable.", "Two are API RP 14E at 100.000000 and 175.000000, and the middle row at 125.000000 is an interpolation the module supplies for service that is neither fully continuous nor fully intermittent.", "Two are API RP 14E, at 100.000000 for continuous and 125.000000 for intermittent service, and the third at 175.000000 is recorded as operator practice."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The third row is labelled clean, inhibited service and carries the operator practice note. All three are overridable, so a company figure can be used directly with its own provenance.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-erosional-limit', 6, 'A caller asks the table for a service id it does not carry, such as a sand laden duty. What comes back?', '["The continuous row, under its own id and its own label at c 100.000000, so a caller reading the label sees which row it got and a caller reading only the c factor sees no sign of the substitution.", "An object carrying an error string naming the id it was given, which is what every other unknown-name lookup in this chain returns and is why the label can be trusted to match the request.", "A null, so that a caller can test for the failure without having to compare a returned label against the string it asked for, which is the cheaper of the two checks to write.", "The most conservative row the table carries, which is the continuous row at 100.000000, deliberately chosen and labelled as a substitution so that the answer errs on the safe side."]'::jsonb, 0, 'It does not throw, it does not come back empty, and it does not carry the name it was asked for. The label is the guard here, and it works only for a caller who reads it.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-erosional-limit', 7, 'The three c factors are held for the literature in this course. What does that mean for a graded question?', '["Graded questions avoid the erosional check entirely and confine themselves to the pressure drop chain.", "Every graded erosional value in this course states its own c factor, so the c factor is a stated condition rather than something a learner is expected to know.", "Graded questions use the continuous row at 100.000000 by convention, since it is the most conservative of the three and the one the recommended practice puts first in its own table.", "Graded questions state the erosional velocity itself, so the learner reads the ceiling directly rather than forming it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The recommended practice says its own figures are conservative and the third row is operator practice with no publication behind it. When a c factor is not stated, the honest answer names the one it assumed.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-erosional-limit', 8, 'The erosional ceiling of 13.545709 ft/s is turned back into a rate through the OGBIA bore. What enters that conversion?', '["The friction factor of 0.0218149625 and the length, since a largest rate has to be the rate at which the line both reaches its ceiling and still delivers against the pressure available.", "The density of 54.500000 lb/ft3 a second time, since the ceiling was formed by dividing by its square root and the conversion back to a volumetric rate has to undo that division.", "The flow area of 0.347410 ft2, and then the barrel and the day, giving 72416.9067 bpd. Nothing new enters, so the largest rate is the erosional velocity wearing different units.", "The c factor of 100.000000 again, applied to the flow area rather than to the density, which is why the three rows of the limit table report three different largest rates."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The three largest rates are 72416.9067, 90521.1334 and 126729.5868 bpd at the three c factors, against the 12000.000000 bpd the line actually carries.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-erosional-limit', 9, 'The limit table reports a ratio of 0.165707 and a margin of 83.429284 percent on the same row. What is the relationship between them?', '["The ratio is the velocity against the ceiling and the margin is the room left in the pressure budget, so one belongs to the erosional check and the other to the loss.", "The margin is the ratio expressed against the erosional velocity of 13.545709 ft/s rather than against the line velocity, which is why one is a fraction and the other a percentage.", "The ratio belongs to the c factor on its own row and the margin is taken against the continuous row throughout, so the margin is comparable between rows in a way the ratio is not.", "They are the same fact stated twice, one as the fraction of the ceiling used and the other as the room left underneath it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Both belong to the c factor on their own row. The margin runs 83.429284, 86.743427 and 90.531019 percent as the ceiling rises, while the line velocity sits still at 2.244621 ft/s.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-erosional-limit', 10, 'Across the three rows of the OGBIA limit table, what stays fixed and what moves?', '["The line velocity of 2.244621 ft/s stays fixed, because the duty and the bore did not change, and the ceiling it is measured against is what moves.", "The ceiling of 13.545709 ft/s stays fixed, because it is set by the density, and the line velocity is what moves.", "Both stay fixed and only the ratio moves, because the ratio is reported against the continuous row whatever c factor the call was made at, which is what makes the three rows comparable.", "The margin stays fixed at 83.429284 percent, because the room underneath the ceiling is a property of the duty."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The ceilings are 13.545709, 16.932137 and 23.704991 ft/s and the ratios are 0.165707, 0.132566 and 0.094690. Every column after the line velocity follows from the c factor.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-erosional-limit', 11, 'What is wrong with reading 72416.9067 bpd as the design capacity of the OGBIA bore?', '["It is a rate at line conditions rather than at stock tank conditions, so it overstates what a tank farm would actually receive once the crude has been taken back to standard.", "It is computed at the clean inhibited c factor of 175.000000, which is the most generous row in the table, so an ordinary continuous-service line is allowed rather less than it states.", "It is the rate at which one specific check stops being satisfied, at one stated c factor, and a line has to satisfy every check it is subject to rather than the most generous one.", "It is the rate at which the ratio reaches 0.165707 rather than 1.000000, so it is the design point the line was actually built at rather than any kind of ceiling on it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Nothing in the erosional check looked at pressure at all. The pressure drop at 72416.9067 bpd would be an entirely different number and would have to be affordable as well.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-erosional-limit', 12, 'The erosional velocity is 13.545709 ft/s on every row of the twelve-bore sweep. Why, and what changes instead?', '["Because the sweep holds the ceiling at the value computed for the built bore of 7.981000 in, which is an approximation the engine makes so that the twelve rows stay comparable with each other.", "Because it depends on the density and the c factor and not on the bore. What changes down the table is the velocity that has to sit under it.", "Because every row carries the same 12000.000000 bpd and the ceiling is formed from the rate and the density.", "Because the ratio column is what actually varies and the ceiling is printed once and repeated, so the figure on each row after the first is a display convenience rather than a separate result."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The velocity runs from 38.027872 ft/s down to 0.635441 ft/s across the twelve bores while the ceiling sits still. The ratio column is the velocity column rescaled by that one figure.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-erosional-limit', 13, 'Name the inputs the pressure drop has to be given that the erosional check never asks for.', '["The density of 54.500000 lb/ft3 and the c factor, since the ceiling is built from the velocity and the flow area of 0.347410 ft2 while the loss is what has to be told what the fluid weighs.", "The velocity of 2.244621 ft/s and the flow area, since the erosional check works from the rate and the bore directly and never forms the velocity the pressure chain had already computed.", "The bore alone, since the ceiling of 13.545709 ft/s is quoted per unit of flow area and the loss is the only one of the two that has to be told how wide the passage actually is.", "The length, the roughness and the viscosity, the last of the three reaching the loss through the Reynolds number of 48431.2523."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The erosional check is handed the rate, the bore, the density and a c factor, and nothing else. Everything the loss needs beyond those three is a question about the pipe and the fluid on the way through it rather than about the wall.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-erosional-limit', 14, 'The OGBIA line is lengthened and everything else is held. What happens to the 25.660631 psi and to the 13.545709 ft/s?', '["The loss grows, because the friction term follows the length directly, and the ceiling does not move at all, because no length appears in it.", "Both grow, because a longer line spends more pressure and the check reads the velocity at the outlet.", "The loss grows and the ceiling falls, because a longer line gives the crude more wall to act on and the check tightens in proportion to the exposure the wall receives over the route.", "Neither moves, because the engine reports both per unit length, so a longer line changes the total and leaves the gradient and the ceiling exactly where they were on the shorter one."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Lengthening the line is a change to the pressure budget and not to the wall check. That is the cleanest statement of the two being different questions asked of one velocity.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-erosional-limit', 15, 'The 2 in schedule 40 row comes back at an erosional ratio of 2.470444. How does that verdict differ in kind from a total the pressure budget cannot meet?', '["It does not differ in kind, since either can be answered by raising the c factor to the clean inhibited row at 175.000000.", "The ratio is the softer of the two, since 2.470444 is a caution attached to a row that still returns every one of its numbers.", "A loss is a cost and a cost can be traded against pumping, against the route or against the duty. A ratio above 1.000000 is a verdict about the wall and there is nothing in it to trade.", "The length, since the ratio is evaluated over the whole route and shortening the run brings the velocity down in proportion."]'::jsonb, 2, 'On this duty that row also spends 20803.315944 psi, so the two criteria happen to agree. The answers to the ratio are a larger bore, a different c factor with a reason behind it, or a different fluid.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-choosing-a-bore', 1, 'The sizing sweep runs the OGBIA duty through twelve bores. What does it hold still?', '["The rate of 12000.000000 bpd, the crude at 54.500000 lb/ft3 and 2.500000 cp, the 26400.000000 ft of length and the roughness. The bore is the only thing that moves.", "Everything except the bore and the roughness, since each nominal size carries its own wall condition with it.", "The velocity, which is held at the design figure while the bore and the rate move together down the table.", "The pressure drop, which is held at the budget the line was designed to, so each row reports a rate."]'::jsonb, 0, 'That is what makes the sweep readable. A designer looking at 20803.315944 psi against 1.236026 psi is looking at one decision rather than at several tangled together.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-choosing-a-bore', 2, 'Across the twelve bores, which row carries the highest velocity, and which the lowest?', '["The highest is 33.463911 ft/s at 2.067000 in, which is the first row of the table and the smallest nominal size the vendored schedule carries, and the lowest is 0.635441 ft/s at 15.000000 in.", "The highest is 38.027872 ft/s at 1.939000 in, and the lowest is 1.003216 ft/s at 11.938000 in, since the 16 in row is outside the range the erosional check was evaluated over.", "The highest is 38.027872 ft/s at a bore of 1.939000 in, which is the 2 in schedule 80 row, and the lowest is 0.635441 ft/s at 15.000000 in.", "The highest is 15.189621 ft/s at 3.068000 in, the widest bore still failing the erosional check."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The smallest bore in the table is 1.939000 in and not 2.067000 in, because a heavier schedule is a thicker wall on the same outside diameter. That row also carries the largest total, 28841.350797 psi.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-choosing-a-bore', 3, 'The sweep is ordered by nominal size and then by schedule. What does that ordering do?', '["It puts the bores in ascending order, since a larger nominal size always carries a larger bore whatever the schedule, so a walk down the table is a walk up through the bores.", "It is not the order of the bores. The heavier schedule sits after the lighter one and has the smaller bore, so reading down the table crosses back and forth over the bore a line actually needs.", "It groups the failing rows at the top, since the erosional check fails on the smallest bores and the table is sorted so that a reader meets the disqualified candidates first.", "It has no effect on anything, since the sweep returns twelve independent rows and the order they are printed in cannot change which of them a designer chooses."]'::jsonb, 1, 'In table order the bores run 2.067000, 1.939000, 3.068000, 4.026000, 3.826000, 6.065000, 5.761000, 7.981000, 7.625000, 10.020000, 11.938000 and 15.000000. Code that stops at the first acceptable row has picked a row.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-choosing-a-bore', 4, 'How many of the twelve bores pass the erosional check on this duty, and which fail?', '["Seven pass, which are the rows whose velocity also sits under the 6.000000 ft/s ceiling, since the erosional check and the velocity ceiling remove the same five candidates from the table.", "Ten pass, since only the two 2 in rows exceed the ceiling of 13.545709 ft/s, and the 3 in row at 15.189621 ft/s is inside it once the clean inhibited c factor is applied to the crude.", "Twelve pass, since every row returns a finite ratio against the ceiling, and the boolean column reports whether the row was evaluated rather than whether it satisfied the limit.", "Nine pass. The three that fail are the smallest bores, at ratios of 2.470444, 2.807374 and 1.121360."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The 2 in schedule 40 row runs at 33.463911 ft/s, the 2 in schedule 80 at 38.027872 ft/s and the 3 in at 15.189621 ft/s, all above the 13.545709 ft/s ceiling.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-choosing-a-bore', 5, 'A velocity ceiling of 6.000000 ft/s is applied alongside the erosional limit, and 7 of the 12 bores pass both. Which check removes the two 4 in rows?', '["The velocity ceiling. They run at 8.820843 and 9.767147 ft/s, which is above 6.000000 ft/s, and both are inside the erosional limit at ratios of 0.651191 and 0.721051.", "The erosional limit, since both 4 in rows sit above the ceiling of 13.545709 ft/s once the velocity is measured at the smaller schedule 80 bore of 3.826000 in rather than at 4.026000 in.", "Both checks together, since the two criteria agree on every row of this sweep.", "Neither check, since the two 4 in rows are among the seven that pass and the 6 in rows are removed instead."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The velocity ceiling is a practice rather than a result of this method. Applied alongside the erosional limit it removes bores the erosional check alone would have allowed, which is the point of it.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-choosing-a-bore', 6, 'Among the bores that pass both criteria, two rows each have a claim to being the answer. Which are they?', '["The 8 in schedule 40 row at 7.981000 in, which is the bore the line was actually built at, and the 6 in schedule 40 row at 6.065000 in, which is the cheapest row that passes both criteria.", "The 4 in schedule 40 row at 4.026000 in and the 16 in row at 15.000000 in, the cheapest on pressure.", "The first in TABLE order, which is 6 in schedule 40 at a bore of 6.065000 in, and the smallest passing BORE, which is 6 in schedule 80 at 5.761000 in.", "The 6 in schedule 40 row at 6.065000 in and the 8 in schedule 40 row at 7.981000 in, which are the two rows whose totals of 97.306913 psi and 25.660631 psi bracket a typical pressure budget."]'::jsonb, 2, 'On this duty they are different rows. The first is the smallest nominal size that works, which is what a buyer wants, and the second is the tightest bore that works.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-choosing-a-bore', 7, 'A pipe size is two numbers plus what is left. Which of the three does the hydraulics read?', '["The outside diameter, since that is what the flanges, the supports and the fittings are built to, and the fittings are what the resistance sum on the isometric was counted from.", "The bore and the wall together, since the wall thickness sets how much of the outside diameter is steel and the friction factor reads the wall through the relative roughness term.", "All three, since the schedule row supplies the outside diameter to the erosional check, the wall to the pressure rating and the bore to the velocity, and the sweep reports all three columns.", "The bore, and only the bore."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A 6 in line is 6.625000 in on the outside in either schedule, because that is what the flanges are built to. The heavier schedule spends its extra steel inwards and the bore is what is left.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-choosing-a-bore', 8, 'A line is uprated to a heavier schedule for reasons of pressure. What happens to it hydraulically at the same duty?', '["Nothing changes, since the outside diameter is unaltered at 8.625000 in and the hydraulics reads the outside diameter, with the wall entering only the pressure rating and the corrosion allowance.", "It becomes a faster line. On the 8 in rows the bore falls from 7.981000 in to 7.625000 in and the velocity rises from 2.244621 ft/s to 2.459110 ft/s.", "It becomes a slower line, since the extra steel is added outwards and the flow area is unchanged.", "It becomes a faster line, but only on the small sizes, since the wall thickness grows in proportion to the nominal size and the fractional loss of bore is therefore the same on every row."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Every heavy-schedule bore in the table is smaller than its light-schedule partner and every heavy-schedule velocity is higher. The pressure drop and the erosional ratio both move with it.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-choosing-a-bore', 9, 'A line is specified as 6 in. What does that name fix, and what does it leave open?', '["It fixes the outside diameter at 6.625000 in and the fittings that go with it, and it leaves the bore open: 6.065000 in in schedule 40 and 5.761000 in in schedule 80.", "It fixes the bore at 6.000000 in, which is what the nominal size states, and leaves the outside diameter open, since that grows with the wall thickness the schedule specifies for the service.", "It fixes both, since a nominal size and the vendored schedule together name exactly one row of the table.", "It fixes the wall thickness at 0.280000 in, and leaves the outside diameter and the bore to follow from it."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Neither of the two 6 in bores is 6.000000 in. The nominal size is a label that fixes the outside diameter, and the bore underneath it follows from the schedule.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-choosing-a-bore', 10, 'The sweep reports a ratio column beside the velocity column. What is it?', '["The velocity divided by the 6.000000 ft/s practice ceiling, which is why the boundary between a passing and a failing row sits at 1.000000 and why seven of the twelve rows fall below it.", "The total pressure drop of each row divided by the drop on the row the line was built at, so the 8 in schedule 40 row reads 0.165707 against itself and the others are quoted relative to it.", "The bore of each row divided by the largest bore in the table, which is 15.000000 in, so the ratio falls as the bore opens and a reader can compare rows without reading two columns.", "The velocity column rescaled by the ceiling of 13.545709 ft/s."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The ceiling is the same on every row because it depends on the density and the c factor rather than on the bore. The ratio is the form in which a velocity becomes a verdict.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-choosing-a-bore', 11, 'What can the sweep not decide on its own?', '["What each bore spends in pressure, since the total column is reported only for the row the line was built at and the other eleven rows carry a velocity and a ratio alone.", "Whether the duty is right, since the sweep holds the rate at 12000.000000 bpd throughout and a line that is oversized for its duty reports the same twelve rows as one that is correctly loaded.", "Which bore to build. It is twelve answers to the question of what each bore costs, and choosing needs the erosional verdict, a view on velocity, and a price the table does not carry.", "Which rows pass the erosional check, since the boolean column depends on a c factor the sweep takes as given and the three published rows are held for the literature in this course."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Moving from 8.561327 psi to 3.689918 psi to 1.236026 psi saves real pressure, and each step is a heavier and dearer pipe buying less than the step before it. Nothing in the table prices that.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-choosing-a-bore', 12, 'Four wall conditions are run against the same OGBIA duty. What is the spread in the friction loss, and what does it say?', '["From 24.781425 psi on new plastic at 0.000060 in to 27.565372 psi on used steel at 0.006000 in, so on this line, at this Reynolds number, the wall is a modest influence.", "From 24.781425 psi to 27.565372 psi, which shows that roughness is always a second-order effect on a liquid line.", "From 24.854515 psi on internally coated pipe to 25.660631 psi on commercial steel, since the used steel row is a projection of wall condition over the line''s life rather than a catalogue entry.", "From 0.0000075179 to 0.0007517855, which is the relative roughness rather than the loss at all."]'::jsonb, 0, 'That is a fact about this row rather than a general one. The friction factor''s sensitivity to roughness depends on where the line is sitting, and a small spread here is not a licence to guess.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-choosing-a-bore', 13, 'The catalogue stores an absolute roughness. What does the friction factor want instead, and what does the division mean?', '["A height in feet rather than inches, which is why the division is by twelve, and the same steel is equally rough in any bore because the bumps themselves are what the wall term reads.", "A fraction of the bore, so 0.001800 in in a 7.981000 in bore becomes 0.0002255356, and the same steel is hydraulically rougher in a small pipe than in a large one.", "An average of the catalogue rows weighted by the pipe''s age, since a line specified on new steel drifts toward the used row over its life and the friction factor reads the drifted figure.", "A roughness Reynolds number, formed from the bumps and the velocity rather than from the bore."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Used steel at 0.006000 in gives 0.0007517855 in the same bore. The bumps are the same height and the pipe they sit in is narrower, so they take up more of it.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-choosing-a-bore', 14, 'A line costed on commercial steel is recomputed years later on the used steel row. What does that move cost, and what was done to the pipe to earn it?', '["24.854515 psi becomes 25.660631 psi, and what earned it is an internal coating wearing off, which is the route by which a coated line arrives at the commercial steel row over its service life.", "Nothing at all, since the catalogue ids describe the material the pipe is made of rather than the condition it is in, and commercial steel stays commercial steel however long a line has run.", "25.660631 psi becomes 24.781425 psi, since deposits smooth the wall of a crude line over its life and the effective roughness falls rather than rises across the years the line is in service.", "25.660631 psi becomes 27.565372 psi, and nothing was done to the pipe at all."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The two rows differ only in the wall condition assumed for the same pipe. The four ids are catalogue entries rather than measurements of this line, so choosing among them is a statement about its condition.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-choosing-a-bore', 15, 'A measured pressure drop disagrees with the computed one, and the roughness is adjusted until they match. What has that done?', '["It has produced the correct answer by the only route available, since the roughness is the one input in the chain that cannot be measured directly on a buried line in service.", "It has broken the relative roughness, since tuning the absolute figure leaves the bore unchanged and the fraction the correlation reads can no longer be recovered from the catalogue.", "It has turned a measured discrepancy into an assertion about pipe condition that nobody inspected, because the catalogue entry describes a wall.", "It has moved the line onto a different branch, since a tuned roughness can push the iteration past 2100."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The crude reaches the friction factor through the Reynolds number and the pipe reaches it through the relative roughness. Confusing them means answering a question about a fluid by changing the pipe.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 1, 'The Associate tier is built on six numbers about one line. What are they?', '["A flow area, a velocity, a Reynolds number, a relative roughness, a friction factor and a pressure gradient, which is the chain in the order the engine runs it from the bore to the answer.", "A velocity, a Reynolds number, a friction factor, a friction loss, a total loss and an erosional ceiling.", "A rate, a bore, a length, a density, a viscosity and a roughness, which are the six inputs a liquid line call takes and the six the refusal message names when any of them is missing.", "A velocity, a velocity head, a friction loss, a fittings loss, an elevation loss and a total, which is the pressure side of the chain with the three terms the engine keeps apart."]'::jsonb, 1, 'On OGBIA they are 2.244621 ft/s, 48431.2523, 0.0218149625, 25.660631 psi, a total of 25.660631 psi at zero rise, and 13.545709 ft/s at the continuous-service c factor.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 2, 'In the chain the engine runs, what does the friction factor consume and what does it feed?', '["It consumes the velocity and the relative roughness, and it feeds the Reynolds number, which is then compared against the two branch boundaries to settle which regime the line is in.", "It consumes the Reynolds number alone, and it feeds both the friction loss and the erosional ceiling, which is why a change of bore moves the ratio as well as the pressure drop.", "It consumes the velocity head and the length over the bore, and it feeds the gradient, which is the form the engine reports a loss in before the total is assembled from the three terms.", "It consumes the Reynolds number and the relative roughness, and it feeds the loss along with the length."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A bore gives an area, the area and the rate give a velocity, the velocity gives a Reynolds number, the Reynolds number and the relative roughness give a friction factor, and that and the length give the loss.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 3, 'A larger bore is bought for a line whose total carries all three terms. Which of the three does it change?', '["All three, since every term in the return is formed from the velocity that opening the bore lowers.", "The friction alone, since the fittings term is a count taken from the isometric and a count does not change.", "The friction and the fittings, both of which are built on a velocity head that falls with the square of the velocity, and not the elevation.", "The elevation and the friction, since a wider pipe holds a shorter column for the same volume of liquid, and the fittings term is the one that is fixed by the drawing rather than by the pipe."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Open the bore and the velocity falls, so the head falls with its square and both of the terms built on that head fall with it. The height of a static column is not something a pipe diameter can reach.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 4, 'The OGBIA line is rebored from 7.981000 in to 6.065000 in at the same duty. Of the six numbers this tier is built on, how many move?', '["Five of the six move with the bore. Only the erosional ceiling of 13.545709 ft/s stands still, because the bore appears nowhere inside it at all.", "All six, since the ceiling is what the line velocity is measured against and a change of bore moves the velocity first of anything.", "Two, the velocity and the loss, since the Reynolds number and the friction factor are properties of the crude and the total is the loss under another name.", "Three, since the velocity, the Reynolds number and the friction factor move while the loss and the total are recovered from the gradient, which the engine reports per foot."]'::jsonb, 0, 'At 6.065000 in the same duty runs at 3.886834 ft/s and spends 97.306913 psi. No bore appears in the ceiling, which reads a density and a c factor and nothing else.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 5, 'This tier has two properties that the next one does not. Name them.', '["Nothing in it compresses, and nothing in it carries a hill, since a static column belongs to the marched profiles of a later tier.", "Nothing in it compresses, so a barrel entering the line is a barrel leaving it, and nothing in it iterates except the friction factor, which settles before the rest of the chain reads it.", "Nothing in it iterates at all, and nothing in it is read from a vendored table, since the bores and the roughnesses are computed rather than looked up.", "Nothing in it is refused, and nothing in it is held for the literature, since every figure the tier prints is an engine return."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A barrel entering the line is a barrel leaving it, so 2.244621 ft/s stands at both ends. Both properties change in the next tier.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 6, 'Nothing in the OGBIA line compresses, so a barrel entering it is a barrel leaving it. What would change if it did compress?', '["The engine would refuse the line, since a density that varies along the run is one of the eighteen states Section 1 lists as having no answer at all in this method.", "Only the erosional ceiling would move, since it is the one figure in the tier built from the density, and the friction chain reads the density solely through the Reynolds number.", "Nothing in the reported numbers, since the density is taken at the inlet and the loss is reported over the whole line, so compressibility would reach the answer only through the gradient.", "The density would depend on the pressure, which is the quantity being solved for."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The velocity of 2.244621 ft/s stands at both ends because the density of 54.500000 lb/ft3 does. Let the fluid compress and the whole method changes shape.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 7, 'A graded question hands over a line and asks for a friction factor. What has to be read before the figure is used?', '["The regime the engine reports, because the two branches are different laws and the band between them is labelled rather than correlated.", "The relative roughness, because it is formed from the roughness and the bore and a question that states an absolute roughness has not yet stated the quantity the correlation actually reads.", "The c factor, because a graded question always states one and both are reported on every row of the sweep.", "The length, because the friction factor is solved over the length of the pipe it belongs to."]'::jsonb, 0, 'On OGBIA the Reynolds number of 48431.2523 is reported turbulent and the friction factor is 0.0218149625. Inside the band from 2100 to 4000 the label and the arithmetic disagree.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 8, 'Which input''s unit is worth checking before any arithmetic, and why that one?', '["The viscosity, because it is the first input the chain consumes, so a centipoise mistaken for another unit is caught before the velocity is ever formed.", "The bore, because a nominal size and a bore are both quoted in inches and both print as ordinary numbers.", "The length, because liquid work here is in feet while gas work is in miles, and the loss scales directly with the length.", "The density, because it enters twice, once in the Reynolds number and once in the velocity head, so a unit error in it moves the friction factor and the loss in the same direction at once."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine accepts 5280.000000000 ft as a rise on a line 1.000000 mile long, so it knows the difference exactly. Both feet and miles are ordinary numbers and the answer comes back wrong by whatever the confusion was worth.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 9, 'Why does a graded erosional question in this course always state its own c factor?', '["Because the three rows at 100.000000, 125.000000 and 175.000000 are overridable, so a question has to say which of them the engine was called with before the answer can be reproduced at all.", "Because the recommended practice gives different c factors for liquid and for gas service, so a question about a crude line has to name which of the two families of figures it intends.", "Because the c factor is the only input to the erosional velocity that the engine does not default, so a question that omitted it would receive a refusal rather than a number from the check.", "Because the three published rows are held for the literature and are never graded."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A learner is never asked to supply one, only to use the one the question carries. An answer worked at a figure of its own is sound arithmetic aimed at a question nobody put, since the three rows give three different ceilings on the same fluid.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 10, 'A velocity comes back from a hand calculation wildly larger than anything the sweep reports. What is the likely cause?', '["The regime was read as turbulent when the line is laminar, since the two branches give friction factors an order apart and the velocity is formed from the friction factor and the loss.", "One of the barrel, the day or the 144 went missing on the way from a rate in bpd to a velocity in ft/s.", "The erosional ceiling was used in place of the line velocity, since both are reported in ft/s and the ceiling of 13.545709 ft/s is several times the velocity of any sensible export line.", "The bore was read from the nominal size rather than from the schedule row."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The chain from 12000.000000 bpd to 2.244621 ft/s runs through a flow area of 0.347410 ft2, the barrel of 5.6145833333333 cubic feet and the 86400.000000 seconds in a day.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 11, 'A friction factor comes back above one. What does that say about the line?', '["That it is laminar, so the regime is worth re-reading. The published case at 150.0000 bpd returns 4.1163843599 at a Reynolds number of 15.5476.", "That the relative roughness was entered as an absolute roughness in inches, since a value of 0.001800 in used directly as a fraction of the bore drives the wall term far past anything physical.", "That the iteration failed to converge, since the turbulent law is solved by repeated substitution.", "That the velocity head was included twice, since a friction factor is dimensionless to begin with."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The turbulent rows of the published cases sit at 0.0260878890, 0.0260794714 and 0.0249265571. The laminar case is the only one of the four whose friction factor is above one, and 4.1163843599 is not a figure the turbulent branch produces anywhere in this tier.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 12, 'A question asks for the friction loss and another asks for the total. When are those different questions?', '["Whenever the line is laminar, since the engine reports the fittings term against the turbulent velocity head.", "Whenever the resistance sum is zero, since the engine then reports the fittings term as absent altogether.", "Whenever a hill or a fitting list is in play. The second published case spends 56.140803 psi of friction inside a total of 153.516220 psi.", "Never, on a liquid line, since the engine keeps the three terms apart in the return and the total is reported for convenience rather than as a separate answer to a separate question."]'::jsonb, 2, 'On the level OGBIA line with no fittings the two are the same 25.660631 psi. Report the total and the terms behind it, because the question will have stated the hill or the list.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 13, 'What does this tier hold for the literature, and what does it say about it?', '["The pipe schedule and the roughness catalogue, since both are vendored tables the engine reads rather than computes, and nothing in this course stands behind the twelve bores or the four wall conditions.", "The band between 2100 and 4000, since the engine computes it on the turbulent branch while labelling it transitional, and a figure computed on the wrong branch cannot be graded in any tier.", "The published liquid cases, since they come from an oracle rather than from a measured pipeline.", "The three c factors, because the recommended practice calls its own figures conservative and the third row is operator practice."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every graded erosional value in this course therefore states its own c factor. The discipline is to say what the method is and not to rest a conclusion on the part of it that nothing stands behind.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 14, 'A report names one bore out of a twelve-row sweep and calls it the recommendation. What has to be stated beside it?', '["Which criteria it applied, and the rule by which it ranked whatever survived them, because on this duty the same sweep defensibly returns two different rows as its answer.", "The pressure that row spends, since a recommendation nobody can check against a budget is not a recommendation and the totals are the only thing a sweep computes.", "The c factor the erosional column was evaluated at, which is the one condition in the whole sweep that came from outside the pressure drop calculation.", "The schedule, since a nominal size on its own does not name a bore, and a report that states the schedule has named exactly one row and needs nothing further."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A ranking is worth what its criteria are worth. Neither of the two applied here knows what the pipe costs, what the yard holds, what wall the rating demands or what the service does to it.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 15, 'The tier''s closing lesson warns against one habit in particular. Which is it, and why?', '["Reading a refusal as a failure of the run, since a refused row stops a sizing sweep where it stands and the remaining bores have to be run again without it.", "Quoting a friction factor without the Reynolds number and the relative roughness behind it, since both of those survive a change of bore and can be carried to any line built of the same steel.", "Carrying a number to another line. Every figure belongs to a complete row, and the method travels where the numbers do not.", "Reporting the friction loss as the pressure drop, since the engine returns that term only on a bare and level line."]'::jsonb, 2, '25.660631 psi belongs to that rate through that bore over that length on that roughness. The same duty through 6.065000 in spends 97.306913 psi.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 1, 'Put the tier''s chain in the order the engine runs it, from the bore to the pressure.', '["Bore to relative roughness, relative roughness to friction factor, friction factor and rate to velocity, and the velocity and the length together to the loss.", "Bore to area, area and rate to velocity, velocity to Reynolds number, Reynolds number and relative roughness to friction factor, friction factor and length to loss.", "Rate to velocity, velocity and density to Reynolds number, Reynolds number to regime, regime to a branch of the friction law, and the branch and the bore together to the loss without an area being formed.", "Bore and length to a length over bore factor, that factor and the roughness to a friction factor, and the friction factor and the rate to the loss."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Every link is the input to the next, so one wrong condition at the top gives a line that is confidently the wrong size at the bottom with every figure on the way down printing normally.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 2, 'A sizing sweep is run over twelve bores and one row is handed a viscosity the guard will not take. What do the other eleven rows return?', '["Their own numbers, because the refusal is an object returned on that one row and it travels back along the same path a result does.", "Nothing, because the guard raises on the offending row and the loop stops there, so the bores below it are never evaluated at all.", "A refusal each, because a sweep puts one fluid through twelve pipes and a fluid the method will not accept disqualifies every row of the table.", "Their own numbers with the regime reported as invalid, because the sweep carries the failed row''s verdict down into the rows beneath it."]'::jsonb, 0, 'That is what a returned error buys. The row that refused can be shown beside the rows that answered, with the reason on it, which a thrown exception would not allow.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 3, 'The OGBIA rate is read as a stock tank figure waiting to shrink rather than as a line-conditions one. Which figures in the row are then wrong?', '["Only the erosional ratio, since it is the one check formed from the rate while the pressure chain reads a velocity that was settled before the rate was interpreted.", "Only the pressure terms, since the velocity and the Reynolds number never read a rate at all.", "Every one of them from the velocity onward, because the whole chain hangs on a rate through an area and the rate was the first thing misread.", "None of them, since the engine takes a rate to line conditions itself using the density of 54.500000 lb/ft3."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Liquid rates here are at LINE conditions, downstream of separation, so 12000.000000 bpd is 12000.000000 bpd of liquid in the pipe. The flow area of 0.347410 ft2 and the 0.001800 in of wall are all that survive the misreading.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 4, 'A drawing gives a liquid line''s run in miles and the figure is handed over unconverted. Which of the tier''s returned numbers gives the mistake away?', '["The regime, because a length thousands of times too small drags the Reynolds number under 2100 and the engine reports the line as laminar when the crude is plainly turbulent at 48431.2523.", "The gradient, because it is the total divided by the length and would be reported in psi per mile, so the unit printed beside it no longer matches the psi per ft the rest of the tier works in.", "None of them. The velocity, the Reynolds number and the friction factor never see the length at all, and the loss is proportional to it, so the answer is simply wrong by whatever the confusion was worth.", "The friction factor, because it is solved over the run of the pipe and a length short by a factor of 5280.000000000 pushes the iteration onto the other branch of the friction law."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine knows the two units apart exactly, accepting 5280.000000000 ft as a rise on a gas line 1.000000 mile long. It is the caller that has to keep them apart on a liquid line, where feet is what is wanted.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 5, 'One barrel is reported as 5.6145833333333 cubic feet, measured out of an engine that does not export it. What question was put to the engine?', '["Its fittings loss of 0.029634 psi at a resistance sum of one, from which the barrel falls out once the density and the velocity have been divided away again.", "Its Reynolds number of 48431.2523 against the velocity and the bore, from which the barrel is what remains once the centipoise has been recovered from the row.", "Nothing was put to it, since a barrel is a definition of volume rather than anything an engine computes, so the published figure is typed in with its source named.", "Its line volume of 1633.5349 bbl, divided into the flow area times the length."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The module keeps that constant to itself, so a figure typed into a teaching file would not be an engine return. gc at 32.174000 and the centipoise at 6.7197000000e-4 are each recovered by a call that isolates them.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 6, 'Of the Reynolds number''s four inputs on the OGBIA line, which reaches the calculation here for the first time?', '["The density of 54.500000 lb/ft3, since the velocity step is a rate and a geometry that would return 2.244621 ft/s for water or for crude alike and never asks what the fluid weighs.", "The bore of 7.981000 in, since the velocity was formed from the rate and the flow area of 0.347410 ft2 rather than from the bore itself, so the inches reach the arithmetic here.", "The viscosity of 2.500000 cp, since the density, the velocity and the bore were all settled before this step.", "The velocity of 2.244621 ft/s, since the caller supplies a rate in bpd and the conversion into a velocity happens inside this expression rather than before it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'This is where the fluid''s character finally reaches the calculation, and 48431.2523 is what it returns. The bore is in the expression twice, directly and inside the velocity, but neither is new here.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 7, 'The OGBIA viscosity is raised to 30.000000 cp. Of the velocity, the Reynolds number, the friction loss and the erosional ratio, which move?', '["All four, since a thicker crude carries more of its own solids into the wall.", "The Reynolds number to 4035.9377 and the loss to 47.087803 psi. The other two do not move at all.", "Three of them, since only the velocity holds at 2.244621 ft/s while the Reynolds number, the loss and the erosional ratio all follow the fluid downward.", "Two of them, the velocity and the Reynolds number, since the loss is proportional to the length and no length was altered on the row."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The ceiling of 13.545709 ft/s reads a density and a c factor, and the velocity is a rate over an area. Neither of those sits downstream of the Reynolds number, so the ratio of 0.165707 is untouched.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 8, 'The engine returns 0.064000000000 at a Reynolds number of 1000.0000 and 0.042666666667 at 1500.0000. What relation do those two figures satisfy?', '["Each is 64.000000 over its own Reynolds number, which is exactly how the laminar numerator is measured back out of an engine that does not export it.", "Each is the one before it scaled by the ratio of the two Reynolds numbers raised to a power the Colebrook expression fixes at a quarter.", "Each is 64.000000 over the square root of its own Reynolds number, which is why the pair falls more slowly than the Reynolds numbers rise.", "Neither satisfies a relation at all, since both are last iterates of a solve and a laminar factor is no more closed form than a turbulent one."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Because the Reynolds number sits underneath, a slower or a thicker flow returns a larger figure, and the walk down that branch reads 0.030490709862, 0.0317150585, 0.042666666667 and 0.0634301170.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 9, 'How is the Colebrook roughness divisor of 3.700000 recovered from an engine that exports no such constant?', '["On a smooth pipe, where the roughness term is exactly zero, from the 0.017989773084 the engine returns at a Reynolds number of 100000.0000.", "From the built row, by dividing the relative roughness of 0.0002255356 into the friction factor of 0.0218149625 that the engine settled on for it.", "It is not recovered at all, since the module exports it beside the two branch boundaries and a caller reads the published figure directly.", "In the fully rough limit, where the Reynolds term falls away: from 0.037903711893 at a relative roughness of 0.010000 and a Reynolds number of 100000000000000.0000."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A constant is measured by choosing inputs that isolate it. The Reynolds numerator of 2.510000 takes the other route, on a smooth pipe where the wall term vanishes instead.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 10, 'The engine reports a line at a Reynolds number of 2500.0000 as transitional. What has it computed?', '["A blend of the laminar and turbulent laws weighted by position in the band, which is why the interior rows fall smoothly from 0.048678586645 at 2100.0000 to 0.043519188769 at 3000.0000.", "The turbulent law, so the word and the arithmetic are saying different things and 0.046053830366 is the turbulent branch''s opinion about a flow that is not reliably turbulent.", "A correlation fitted to the transition band itself, which is what the reported label is announcing and why the band continues neither branch.", "The laminar law with a caution attached, since the engine stays on that branch until 4000 and the label marks where it is least reliable."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The lower boundary is where the discontinuity sits. At 2099.0000 the engine reports laminar and at 2100.0000 transitional, and the upper of that pair is computed on the turbulent branch.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 11, 'Two published cases run through the same 2.067000 in bore. One spends 14.550645 psi over 3000.000000 ft and the other 130.867123 psi over 5000.000000 ft. What separates them?', '["The length alone, since the friction loss follows the length and the dearer case is the longer pipe.", "The elevation, since the cheaper case falls 60.000000 ft and recovers -26.000000 psi on the way down.", "The roughness, since a bore that small makes the wall term carry most of the loss.", "The branch. The first runs at 2.230927 ft/s and a Reynolds number of 35684.4798, and the second at 0.418299 ft/s and 15.5476."]'::jsonb, 3, '0.0249265571 against 4.1163843599. A line slow enough to go laminar can spend more pressure than a far larger duty in a far larger pipe, which is where the habit that velocity and cost move together breaks.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 12, 'The isometric''s 4.500000 velocity heads cost 0.133351 psi where a single head costs 0.029634 psi. What does the arithmetic between those two figures establish?', '["That a fittings loss is one velocity head multiplied by a resistance sum, since 4.500000 heads cost 4.500000 times what a single head costs.", "That a fittings loss carries the length over bore factor as well, since the same list on a 300.000000 ft run also costs 0.133351 psi.", "That a fittings loss is the velocity head raised to the resistance sum, which is why an isometric costs several times what one fitting does.", "That the engine reads the four entries of the isometric separately, since a resistance sum of one is the elbow row and 4.500000 is the whole list."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The isometric costs the single head multiplied by 4.500000, and neither figure survives a change of bore, because the head they are both built on is set by the velocity the duty makes.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 13, 'The same OGBIA isometric is moved onto a line of 6.065000 in bore carrying the same duty. What travels with it, and what does not?', '["Both travel, since a resistance sum is a pressure in psi accumulated from the fitting table and each entry on the list already carries its own cost in it.", "Neither travels, since the K of each fitting is quoted for the bore it was tabulated in, so a change of bore needs a fresh lookup for every entry on the drawing.", "The resistance sum of 4.500000 travels, because it is a count of velocity heads belonging to the shapes on the drawing. The 0.133351 psi does not, because the head it counts belongs to the stream.", "The 0.133351 psi travels and the count does not, since the pressure a fitting costs is a property of the fitting while the count depends on how the drawing was read."]'::jsonb, 2, 'At 6.065000 in the same duty runs at 3.886834 ft/s instead of 2.244621 ft/s, and a velocity head carries the square of the velocity. The count belongs to the shape of the fitting and the pressure belongs to the stream it is placed in.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 14, 'An engineer leaves the isometric out of the calculation entirely. On which of the tier''s two runs is that defensible, and what settles it?', '["On the manifold run, where the pipe spends only 0.291598 psi, so the whole answer is small.", "On the long export line, where the same list is 0.005170 of the total, and what settles it is the length rather than the fittings.", "On neither, since a resistance sum reaches the loss through the same velocity head the pipe friction does, so an omitted term is an omitted term on any length.", "On whichever run is the slower, since a fittings cost is the velocity head times the sum."]'::jsonb, 1, 'One list, unchanged, worth 0.005170 of the total on the one and 0.313805 on the other. The rule that follows is about the length: ask what the pipe is spending first.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 15, 'A 420.000000 ft rise adds 158.958333 psi to the OGBIA line. What would half that rise add, and what would it do to the friction term?', '["Half of it, and the friction term would fall with it, because a gentler climb lets the stream run faster and a faster stream meets less wall for each foot it covers.", "A quarter of it, because a static column carries its height in the same square that a velocity head carries its velocity, and the friction term would not move.", "The same 158.958333 psi, because the engine reports one elevation term for the whole line whatever the profile is, and the friction term would not move.", "Half of it, and nothing at all to the friction term."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The static column is the density times the height. The friction column reads 25.660631 psi on all three rows of the elevation table, because a hill changes nothing about the rate, the bore, the roughness or the viscosity.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 16, 'The OGBIA line dropped 420.000000 ft returns a total of -133.297702 psi. What are the two numbers inside that figure?', '["25.660631 psi of friction, still positive, and -158.958333 psi of static column.", "A friction term the engine declines to report on a descending line, and -158.958333 psi of static column, which is the whole of the figure.", "A friction term the descent has turned negative, and -158.958333 psi of static column, so the total is the sum of two negative numbers.", "25.660631 psi of friction and -158.958333 psi of static column, beside a fittings term the engine suppresses whenever a total comes back negative."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The three terms on that row are 25.660631, 0.000000 and -158.958333 psi. They are returned apart precisely so that the sign on the total can be read back to the hill that produced it.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 17, 'A long route is costed by taking 0.0069931426 psi per ft off a climbing section and scaling it along the whole run. What is wrong with that?', '["Nothing at all, since a gradient is a loss per foot and scaling it along a route is the operation it was reported for.", "The sign, since a route that later falls returns -0.0050491554 psi per ft and no scaling can carry that across.", "The friction part of that average scales with the length and the static column inside it does not, so extending the pipe extends only one of the two terms.", "The regime, since a longer pipe at the same duty sits at a different Reynolds number altogether."]'::jsonb, 2, '184.618965 psi over 26400.000000 ft carries 158.958333 psi of hill inside it. On a level bare line the same figure is 0.0009719936 psi per ft and is the friction alone, which does scale.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 18, 'Which quantities does the API RP 14E erosional velocity take, and which of the line''s does it ignore?', '["It takes a c factor, the density and the bore, because a ceiling on a velocity has to know the passage it applies to.", "It takes a c factor and the fluid density, and it ignores the bore, the length, the roughness, the viscosity and the rate.", "It takes a c factor, the density, the viscosity and the rate, because how much fluid passes the wall matters to erosion as much as how fast and how heavy that fluid happens to be.", "It takes a c factor and the mixture velocity, because the check is a comparison between two velocities and both halves of that comparison have to be formed before either is useful."]'::jsonb, 1, '13.545709 ft/s follows from 54.500000 lb/ft3 and a c factor of 100.000000 and from nothing else, which is why a ceiling can be quoted before a bore has been chosen.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 19, 'A 20.000000 lb/ft3 mixture is allowed 22.360680 ft/s at a c factor of 100.000000. What is it allowed at 125.000000, and what changed in the expression?', '["27.950850 ft/s, and the density as well, since the intermittent row is quoted for a lighter mixture.", "A figure between 22.360680 and 27.950850 ft/s, since the c factor enters beneath the same root.", "27.950850 ft/s, and only the c factor, which multiplies the whole expression while the density under the root stands still.", "22.360680 ft/s still, since an allowance is a property of the mixture and the c factor only selects which of the three rows the check reports the verdict against."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Reading across a row of the density table is reading the c factor alone. Reading down a column is reading the density, and the two are different kinds of judgement.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 20, 'A twelve-bore sweep is run at a c factor id the table does not carry. What appears in the erosional column of all twelve rows?', '["An error string on every row, since an unknown id is one of the eighteen states the method has no answer for and it refuses before a single bore is evaluated.", "The continuous-service ceiling of 13.545709 ft/s, because the lookup resolved to the first row and that is what each bore was then measured against.", "A blank column, since the lookup came back empty and the sweep reports the columns it could compute and leaves out the check it could not.", "The clean inhibited ceiling of 23.704991 ft/s, since an unrecognised service is resolved upward to the most generous row that the table carries."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Twelve rows, one substituted ceiling, and nine of them reading true against it. The returned label is the only place the substitution is visible at all.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 21, 'The OGBIA line reports a margin of 90.531019 percent. What does that margin NOT tell a designer?', '["Anything about the density, since a margin is formed from the c factor and the velocity and the density has already been divided out of both of them.", "Which c factor it was taken at, since the margin column is reported against the continuous row throughout so that the three rows of the table stay comparable.", "Whether the velocity is inside the 6.000000 ft/s practice ceiling, since that ceiling is what a margin is measured from once a sweep applies both criteria at once.", "Anything about the pressure the line spends, which on that row is 25.660631 psi."]'::jsonb, 3, '90.531019 percent is the room under the clean inhibited ceiling of 23.704991 ft/s. Nothing in the erosional check reads a length, a roughness or a friction factor.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 22, 'Which row of the twelve-bore sweep sits closest to the erosional ceiling while still passing it, and at what ratio?', '["The 4 in schedule 80 row at a bore of 3.826000 in, at 0.721051.", "The 4 in schedule 40 row at a bore of 4.026000 in, at 0.651191, since it is the first row down the table whose verdict the check reports as true.", "The 3 in schedule 40 row at a bore of 3.068000 in, at 1.121360, since it is the last row before the check begins reporting true as the table is read downward.", "The 2 in schedule 80 row at a bore of 1.939000 in, at 2.807374, since it carries the highest velocity of the twelve and so stands nearest the ceiling of them all."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The passing ratios run 0.721051, 0.651191, 0.318024, 0.286942, 0.181542, 0.165707, 0.105128, 0.074062 and 0.046911. The largest of them is the tightest margin that still passes.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 23, 'Two rows of the sweep share an outside diameter of 4.500000 in. What separates them, and what does the difference cost?', '["Walls of 0.237000 in and 0.337000 in on bores of 4.026000 in and 3.826000 in, and totals that are identical, with only the erosional ratios differing at 0.651191 and 0.721051.", "Nothing in the hydraulics, since an outside diameter is what the flanges are built to.", "Walls of 0.237000 in and 0.337000 in, so bores of 4.026000 in and 3.826000 in, and totals of 728.175700 psi and 937.455844 psi.", "Two different nominal sizes, since 4.500000 in belongs to the 4 in entry and to the 3 in entry."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The heavier schedule spends its extra steel inwards, so the bore is what is left. A line uprated for pressure quietly becomes a faster and a dearer line at the same duty.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 24, 'Down the twelve bores the velocity falls from 38.027872 ft/s to 0.635441 ft/s while the total falls from 28841.350797 psi to 1.236026 psi. Why does the pressure fall so much further?', '["Because the friction factor falls with the bore as well, so three separate effects compound.", "Because the bore is under the square of the velocity in the head and again in the length over bore factor, so both of the terms standing in front of the friction factor fall as it opens.", "Because the length over bore factor falls with the bore while the velocity head does not, so only one of the two terms in the loss follows the velocity down.", "Because the wide rows are evaluated at a reduced duty rather than at the same 12000.000000 bpd."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Read the velocity column and the total column of the sweep against each other and the totals fall away much the faster of the two. That is why bore is the first thing reached for when a pressure budget will not close.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 25, 'The 6.000000 ft/s practice ceiling is applied on top of the erosional limit. How many bores does each leave, and where does the difference come from?', '["Nine and then seven, and the two rows lost are the 4 in pair at 8.820843 and 9.767147 ft/s, which the erosional limit had allowed.", "Nine and then seven, and the two rows lost are the 6 in pair at 3.886834 and 4.307863 ft/s, which sit above the practice ceiling while inside the published one.", "Twelve and then seven, since the erosional check returns a ratio on every row of the table and it is the practice ceiling that removes a candidate for the first time.", "Seven and then seven, since the two criteria remove the same rows on this duty."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A line can be inside the RP 14E ceiling and still run faster than an operator wants to run crude. Their erosional ratios of 0.651191 and 0.721051 were both comfortable.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 26, 'Used steel at 0.006000 in gives a relative roughness of 0.0007517855 in the OGBIA bore. What do the friction factor and the loss do?', '["They do not move, since 0.0007517855 is small enough to leave the line hydraulically smooth and the Reynolds number of 48431.2523 carries the whole answer.", "The factor rises to 0.0234342460 and the loss falls to 24.854515 psi, since a rougher wall slows the stream and a slower stream spends less pressure over the run.", "They rise, from 0.0218149625 to 0.0234342460 and from 25.660631 psi to 27.565372 psi.", "They fall, to 0.0210675199 and 24.781425 psi, since the catalogue runs roughest to smoothest."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Used steel at 0.006000 in is the roughest of the four catalogue rows and commercial steel at 0.001800 in the second roughest. Across the whole catalogue this line lands between 24.781425 psi and 27.565372 psi.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 27, 'The same 12000.000000 bpd is put through 6.065000 in instead of 7.981000 in. Give the velocity, the loss and the erosional ratio on that row.', '["3.886834 ft/s, 97.306913 psi and 0.165707, since an erosional ratio is a property of the crude and its c factor rather than of the bore chosen to carry it.", "3.886834 ft/s, 25.660631 psi and 0.286942, since a loss belongs to the duty and the length and the change of bore touched neither of those two.", "4.307863 ft/s, 125.058374 psi and 0.318024, which is the 6 in row of the sweep at the schedule the OGBIA line was actually built to.", "3.886834 ft/s, 97.306913 psi and 0.286942."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every figure in the tier belongs to a complete row. The ceiling of 13.545709 ft/s is the one number that did not move, because no bore appears anywhere in it.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 28, 'Colebrook is iterated with no convergence flag returned. What does the tier offer instead as evidence that the settled iterate is right?', '["A residual reported beside the friction factor, which a caller reads to see how far the last pass moved before the engine stopped iterating on it.", "Four published cases where the engine and the golden agree digit for digit, at 0.037504518014, 0.021926421295, 0.019943465840 and 0.010859745054.", "The regime, which the engine returns as invalid whenever the iteration has not settled inside the number of passes it allows itself.", "The laminar branch, which is closed form, so a turbulent factor is checked by evaluating 64.000000 over the same Reynolds number and comparing the two."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Those four span Reynolds 5000.0000 to 10000000.0000 and relative roughness 0.000050 to 0.001000. Two independent implementations landing on the same digits is what the chain''s standing rests on.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 29, 'A sizing sweep varies the bore and holds everything else still. What makes so small a change of it move so much?', '["Because it is the only input a designer is free to choose, since the rate, the density and the viscosity all arrive from outside the calculation and cannot be altered by a sizing decision.", "Because it enters the friction loss through the length over bore factor, which is the term the length sits in as well, so the two strongest influences on the loss share one factor.", "Because the area goes as the square of the bore, so the velocity moves by that square too, and the velocity is what four separate answers are then built on.", "Because it fixes the relative roughness, and the relative roughness is the axis of the Moody chart along which the friction factor moves furthest at any given Reynolds number."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A bore of 7.981000 in encloses 0.347410 ft2 and a bore twice as wide encloses four times that. One duty through 1.939000 in runs at 38.027872 ft/s and through 15.000000 in at 0.635441 ft/s.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 30, 'Two of the bore''s routes into the friction loss stand outside the friction factor entirely. Name them, and say what the sweep shows for it.', '["Through the relative roughness and through the Reynolds number, and across three neighbouring bores the loss falls from 97.306913 psi to 25.660631 psi to 8.561327 psi as the wall term relaxes.", "Through the length over bore factor and through the velocity head, and across three neighbouring bores the loss falls from 97.306913 psi to 25.660631 psi to 8.561327 psi.", "Through the flow area alone, which carries the bore into the velocity and thereby into every term downstream of it.", "Through the length over bore factor alone, since the velocity head is built from the density and a velocity the duty had already fixed."]'::jsonb, 1, '6.065000 in, 7.981000 in and 10.020000 in are within a few inches of each other and the cost falls away steeply across them. The bore is inside the friction factor as well, through the Reynolds number and the relative roughness, and those are the two routes this question set aside.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 31, 'The pressure drop and the erosional check are run on the OGBIA line. Which computed quantity do they both read?', '["The velocity of 2.244621 ft/s, formed from the duty and the flow area of 0.347410 ft2.", "The friction factor of 0.0218149625, which reaches the loss through the length over bore factor and reaches the erosional check through the ceiling that the ratio is taken against.", "The viscosity of 2.500000 cp, which the loss reads through the Reynolds number of 48431.2523 and the erosional check reads through the ceiling of 13.545709 ft/s.", "Nothing that either of them computed, since the erosional check works from the rate and the bore and never forms a velocity at all, comparing the rate against the largest rate the bore allows instead."]'::jsonb, 0, 'They share the density as well, 54.500000 lb/ft3 sitting in the velocity head and again under the root that gives 13.545709 ft/s. The velocity is the computed quantity both of them read, and the length, the roughness and the viscosity reach only the loss.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 32, 'A bore is chosen on the erosional check alone and the duty raised to the 72416.9067 bpd it allows. What has to be re-formed, and what has not moved?', '["Only the friction factor moves, since the velocity head is built from the density and the loss follows the length, so a change of rate reaches the answer through the friction factor alone.", "Nothing moves, since the two checks read one velocity and a rate that satisfies either is priced.", "Only the ceiling moves, since it is the figure the rate was chosen against, and the loss of 25.660631 psi was settled by the bore and the length before any duty was put through them.", "The velocity, the Reynolds number, the friction factor and the loss all move with the rate, and the ceiling of 13.545709 ft/s does not move at all."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Nothing in the erosional check reads a length, a roughness or a friction factor, so the loss at that rate is an entirely separate number and would have to be affordable on its own.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 33, 'The 3 in schedule 40 row reads a ratio of 1.121360 and the 4 in schedule 40 row reads 0.651191. What lies between those two rows, and what does it buy?', '["One schedule, since 3.068000 in and 4.026000 in are the heavy and light walls of a single outside diameter, and dropping a schedule is what carries the verdict from false to true.", "One nominal size. The bore goes from 3.068000 in to 4.026000 in, the velocity from 15.189621 ft/s to 8.820843 ft/s, the total from 2820.384450 psi to 728.175700 psi, and the verdict from false to true.", "One c factor, since the 3 in row is read against continuous service and the 4 in row against the clean inhibited figure of 175.000000, which is what lifts the ceiling past the row''s velocity.", "Nothing the sweep varies, since both rows carry the same 12000.000000 bpd over the same 26400.000000 ft, so the whole of the difference between 2820.384450 psi and 728.175700 psi is the friction factor."]'::jsonb, 1, 'One step up the nominal sizes is where this duty crosses out of the failing rows. What it costs is a dearer pipe, and the sweep carries no price for that at all.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 34, 'What does the twelve-bore sweep hold still, and why does that matter to the reader?', '["The velocity, at the figure the design was drawn to, so each row reports the duty that bore would carry at an acceptable speed rather than what one fixed duty costs in it.", "The duty, the fluid, the length and the roughness, so every difference down the table is the bore''s doing and 20803.315944 psi against 1.236026 psi is one decision rather than several tangled together.", "The pressure drop, at the budget the system has available, so each row reports the rate that bore would pass inside that budget and the twelve rows are twelve capacities.", "Everything except the bore and the roughness, since each nominal size is drawn from its own part of the vendored schedule and carries the wall condition recorded against it there."]'::jsonb, 1, 'That is what makes a sweep readable. One duty, twelve pipes, and the velocity spans the table from 38.027872 ft/s to 0.635441 ft/s because the crude and the rate never moved.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 35, 'The engine returns 0.039909964901 at a Reynolds number of 3999.0000 and 0.039907014056 at 4000.0000, calling the first transitional and the second turbulent. What does that pair prove?', '["That the engine blends the two laws through the band and the blend has reached the turbulent law by 4000, which is why the two rows so nearly agree with each other.", "That a discontinuity sits at the upper boundary as well as at the lower one, and is simply too small to see at the four decimal places those two rows agree to.", "That the label changed and the arithmetic did not, because both rows were computed on the same branch.", "That the turbulent law is continuous with the laminar one at 4000, by design."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The step is at the lower boundary, where the smooth pipe goes from 0.030490709862 to 0.048678586645 and the OGBIA line jumps 60.303955 percent across one unit of Reynolds number.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 36, 'The three c factor rows are all described as overridable. What does that buy an operating company?', '["A figure the engine validates against the published rows before it accepts one, so an override cannot stray far from RP 14E without the check reporting that it has.", "A fourth row in the table under an id of its own, so that the three published figures and the company figure can all be swept against one another in a single call.", "Very little, since an override is refused for continuous service, the row the lookup falls back to.", "A company figure used directly, carrying its own provenance, with the two RP 14E rows left exactly as published."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A c factor is a judgement about a fluid and a service life. Expressing a local view as an override rather than as a quiet edit to the table is what keeps the published rows readable.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 37, 'On the 20000.0000 bpd published case the golden returns a Reynolds number of 20644.5892 against the engine''s 20644.5578. What follows from a disagreement that size?', '["Almost nothing. The friction factors are 0.0260794714 and 0.0260794621 and the totals 153.516220 psi and 153.516115 psi.", "That the two are on different branches, since that Reynolds number sits near a boundary.", "That the golden was computed over a different length, since 52800.000000 ft is the one input of the case that the two implementations state in different units.", "That neither figure can be quoted at all, since two implementations disagreeing in the fourth decimal have not validated one another in any useful sense."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Two independent implementations in two unit systems land within a small fraction of a psi across all four published liquid cases. That is what gives the chain its standing.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 38, 'Eighteen states have no answer in this method. What do all eighteen have in common?', '["Each is a physical impossibility rather than a fault in the shape of the input, which is why a traverse handed no profile segment is reported through a different mechanism from the others.", "Each comes back as an object carrying an error string, so a caller checks a property rather than catching anything.", "Each raises an exception that names itself, so code that forgets to catch one stops rather than carrying a defaulted value forward into the rest of a sizing sweep unnoticed.", "Each returns a result with its numeric fields set to 0.000000, so a refusal is told apart from an answer by the arithmetic rather than by any property on the returned object."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A returned error travels back along the same path as a result, so it can be shown beside the other rows of a sizing sweep with the row that refused saying why.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 39, 'A routine walks the sweep and returns the first row whose erosional verdict is true. What has it actually found?', '["The smallest bore that passes, since the table is ordered by nominal size and the bores rise steadily along with it.", "The cheapest passing pipe, since the totals rise as the table is read downward and the first row to pass is therefore the last one still affordable.", "A row rather than a bore. The very next line of the table carries a tighter bore than the one it stopped on, and that row passes as well.", "Nothing usable, since the erosional verdict is a boolean and a walk that stops at the first true row cannot report a ratio for the rows it skipped."]'::jsonb, 2, 'In table order the bores run 2.067000, 1.939000, 3.068000, 4.026000, 3.826000 and onwards. A heavier schedule is a smaller bore and it sits after the lighter one.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 40, 'At a relative roughness of 0.010000 the engine returns 0.038503543527 at Reynolds 100000.0000 and 0.037904323387 at 100000000.0000. What has happened to that pipe?', '["The correlation has run past the roughness range it was published over, so both figures are extrapolations and the engine reports the regime as invalid on each of them.", "The pipe has gone hydraulically smooth, since a large relative roughness saturates the wall term and the Reynolds number then carries the whole of the answer between them.", "The two columns have crossed, since a rough pipe returns the larger friction factor at the higher Reynolds number while a smooth one returns it at the lower.", "The wall has taken over, so a thousandfold change in the rate barely moves the answer."]'::jsonb, 3, 'On the smooth row the same two columns are 0.017989773084 and 0.005940466352. A rough pipe stops caring about the Reynolds number and a smooth one never does.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 41, 'The largest rate the OGBIA bore is allowed runs 72416.9067, 90521.1334 and 126729.5868 bpd across the three rows. What did not change to produce that?', '["The bore, its flow area of 0.347410 ft2 and the density of 54.500000 lb/ft3. Only the c factor moved.", "The density alone. The flow area is re-evaluated on every row, because a largest rate is the rate at which that particular bore arrives at its own ceiling.", "Nothing at all. A largest rate is formed from the c factor, the density and the bore together, so all three had to be restated for each of the three rows.", "The c factor. The three rates belong to three fluids at one continuous-service ceiling."]'::jsonb, 0, 'One bore, one crude, three services, and ceilings of 13.545709, 16.932137 and 23.704991 ft/s. The rate the line actually carries is 12000.000000 bpd, which is 0.165707 of the first of the three.'),
  ('linesizing', 'beginner', 'final', null, 42, 'The OGBIA duty and crude are put through the same 7.981000 in bore over 300.000000 ft instead of 26400.000000 ft. Which reported figures are unchanged?', '["The total of 25.660631 psi, since the level row reports no fittings and no elevation and a total built from one term is a property of the fluid.", "The velocity of 2.244621 ft/s, the Reynolds number of 48431.2523 and the friction factor of 0.0218149625.", "The friction loss of 25.660631 psi, since a loss belongs to the fluid and the bore.", "None of them, since every figure in the chain reads the length, which is why a loss cannot be scaled along a route without the chain being run again."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The loss is not among them: 25.660631 psi becomes 0.291598 psi, because the friction term follows the length directly while nothing upstream of it does.');
